window.harnessChunks["rows-2d1260b1c4811f0b.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-29T15:50:49Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/QwenAudio/qwen-audio-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Qwen Audio Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-29\">2026-09-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/QwenAudio/qwen-audio-agent/tree/7b5913a05666a6cab9681c301807206ffa66c106\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7b5913a05666a6cab9681c301807206ffa66c106\">7b5913a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: conduct realtime user-facing assistance by interpreting current conversation/context, choosing a substantive response or available frontend action, and incorporating returned tool/task results into later responses. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user intent, conversational context, available capabilities, tool/retrieval results, task status/results, pending clarification/permission state, attachments and persistent personalization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose whether to answer directly, invoke an available specialized frontend tool, submit sustained work through spawnthinking, ask a necessary question, or incorporate a returned result into the next response. - Decision owner: the configured Realtime model acting as the frontend agent under the shipped first-party prompt/tool contract. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Gateway Realtime transport, frontend tool registry, capability gating, task/status/permission envelopes, conversation/session persistence, memory context and deterministic tool execution. - Closure path: user/context input → Realtime model selects response/tool action → first-party Gateway executes or routes the action → result/state returns into model context → later model response/action. - Boundary reachability: the Realtime frontend and tool registry are the standard shipped user-facing runtime and remain operational even in frontend-only mode; no backend-private agent or repository-development actor is needed for this S1 path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing model discretion while retaining the Gateway/tool machinery eliminates the substantive choice among direct response, tool use and delegation; deterministic runtime code validates, routes and executes the selected action rather than making an equivalent conversational/task decision itself. - Evidence: PROMPT.md; frontend-tools.mjs; deep-dive.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external model-provider cognition is outside the repository implementation boundary; the credited autonomy is the supported first-party model/context/tool feedback loop around that agent actor. Backend Agent work is not needed to establish S1 and its internal autonomy is not imported.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party coordination function was established that attenuates a specific interference/oscillation among distinct credited S1 operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the candidate S2 disturbance would be destructive interference among multiple autonomous operational units; the reviewed first-party product boundary instead exposes one credited Realtime S1 and one optionally integrated external backend system. - Distinct S1 units: multiple Task records, queued requests and backend Sessions are not automatically S1 units. Backend-private subagents/Sessions belong to the configured external backend unless independently brought inside the declared boundary. - Inter-S1 disturbance: no specific first-party disturbance among two or more credited qwen-audio-agent S1 units was established. - Attenuating coordination relation: owner FIFO scheduling, backend write serialization, task claims and delivery deduplication regulate runtime races and lifecycle ordering but are generic infrastructure mechanisms rather than an established inter-S1 coordination relation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: no function-specific S2 mutual-adjustment closure was established. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the repository explicitly uses queues, Session correlation and delegation to move/order work; Methodology 0.3.6 does not treat those surfaces as S2 without distinct S1 units plus a concrete cross-unit interference witness. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S2. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskScheduler, per-owner FIFO queue, backend serialization lock, renewable delivery claims, Task registry and delegation correlation. - Closure path: not established for an S2 function. - Boundary reachability: not applicable because no positive S2 function is published. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the frontend can delegate work and the external backend can create private Sessions, but task decomposition/routing does not by itself regulate a specific disturbance among qwen-audio-agent operational units. - Evidence: deep-dive.md; task-manager.mjs. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a configured backend may internally contain multiple coordinated agents; those backend-private arrangements are not first-party qwen-audio-agent S2 evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party whole-system current-control function was established over multiple operational units&#39; shared resources, commitments, priorities, accountability or synergy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the Gateway tracks task lifecycle, capacity and permissions, but these do not establish discretionary whole-system regulation of current operations at the assessed recursion. - Whole-system current view: TaskManager can enumerate and persist current Tasks, and the Realtime frontend can query task status, but the architecture intentionally withholds backend Session topology, execution strategy, tools, Agents and subagents from the frontend control surface. - Current-control decision scope: users/models can query or cancel a selected Task and relay explicit permission/input responses; static schedulers enforce capacity. No first-party actor is shown bargaining/revising whole-system resources, priorities or commitments across autonomous operational units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskManager, TaskScheduler, cancellation state machine, permission policy, progress/status projection, backend locks and restart recovery. - Closure path: no S3-level whole-system control loop established. - Boundary reachability: not applicable because no positive S3 function is published. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Realtime model can request status/cancellation but explicitly cannot select/create/continue/cancel backend Sessions, choose execution mode/strategy, or select backend tools/Agents/subagents. Deterministic Gateway controls enforce lifecycle rather than exercise organizational S3 discretion. - Evidence: deep-dive.md; PROMPT.md; task-manager.mjs. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: current-task cancellation and permission approval are real control actions but remain below the S3 threshold because they do not reconstruct a whole-system current-control function at this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary audit path was established that challenges ordinary operational claims and returns findings into subsequent control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the candidate S3 disturbance would be uncertainty that ordinary task/model reporting matches actual operational reality; first-party validation focuses on normal lifecycle integrity rather than a separate complementary audit function. - Claim being audited: Task completion, delegation correlation, permissions, memory-learning evidence and delivery state all receive deterministic validation, but these are ordinary execution/data-integrity claims in their primary paths. - Ordinary reporting path: backend protocol events, Gateway Task records, frontend tool results, model/user conversation and memory-learning observations. - Complementary access path: not established. Exact delegation/result correlation, stopReason completion gating, audit logs and structural preference guards inspect the same operational path or provide diagnostics rather than an independent alternative view of reality. - Independence boundary: no first-party auditor with materially different access from the producing operational path was found in the credited distribution. - Who acts on findings: routine runtime code rejects invalid/stale lifecycle or learning inputs; diagnostic audit records can be inspected by an operator, but no complementary-audit finding loop into S3 is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegation correlation, completion-state validation, memory-audit log, sensitive-content/evidence guards, delivery claims and protocol validation. - Closure path: no complementary S3 closure established. - Boundary reachability: not applicable because no positive S3 function is published. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model- or runtime-based checks inside the normal production/learning path do not become S3 merely because they reject bad state; materially independent access and audit feedback are missing. - Evidence: deep-dive.md; profile-observer.mjs; preference-learning.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: repository tests and scenario validation are adjacent development/evaluation surfaces and are not borrowed as runtime S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: adapt future assistant behaviour to stable user characteristics and long-term interaction preferences learned or explicitly selected across sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user characteristics and preferred interaction style can remain unknown, change, or differ from the instance-wide default; blindly carrying one fixed persona/reply strategy across future sessions would lose environment-relevant variety. - External distinction: the evidence comes from the external user/environment — user utterances and explicit preference settings — rather than from internal task-planning state alone. - Future / prospective distinction: accepted observations/preferences are persisted specifically to influence later interactions; inferred promotions are not injected back into the just-ended session, and direct profile edits are documented to apply to subsequent voice sessions. - Adaptation option generated: in autonomous mode, ProfileObserver uses a text-model call to infer bounded candidate values for occupation, special skills, response length and response style from actual user utterances; candidates are cross-session-confirmed and promoted into the observed section of USER.md. In parent mode, the user/operator explicitly selects a durable personalization change such as preferred name/language/reply style/default behavior. - Path back into current capability / S3: promoted or explicit USER.md content is composed into later frontend instructions as &lt;userpreferences&gt; and has higher personalization precedence than the default assistant profile, changing later Realtime model behaviour; explicit tool writes can update the active preference context while direct file edits apply on later sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: base mode — the observer model makes the substantive semantic inference from user evidence, while deterministic quote/field guards and multi-session promotion thresholds validate/enforce when that option may become durable. Parent mode — the legitimate user/operator directly decides the long-term personalization setting/correction and first-party memory machinery returns it into future frontend context. - Decision owner: base mode — model-driven ProfileObserver; parent mode — current user/operator for their durable personalization overlay. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: candidate pool/store, evidence anchoring guards, ≥2-confirmation/≥2-session promotion gate, PreferencePromoter, owner-serialized memory writes, USER.md, memory provider/runtime and frontend context composition. - Closure path: completed sessions → model observer infers candidate from user evidence → candidate accumulates independent cross-session confirmations → promoter persists accepted preference → later frontend context loads preference → subsequent agent responses operate under the adapted preference. Parent mode: explicit durable preference/correction → memory tool/API/direct edit → persisted USER.md → subsequent frontend context/behaviour. - Boundary reachability: both paths are first-party documented product modes at the frozen revision. Autonomous preference learning is optional/off by default but enabled through the supported QWENAUDIOPREFERENCELEARNING=on configuration; explicit long-term personalization is part of the standard conversational/product interface. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in the autonomous mode, removing the observer model while leaving storage, guards and thresholds intact eliminates the substantive inference that proposes what environmental/user distinction should change future behaviour; deterministic machinery validates and promotes that model-generated option rather than inventing an equivalent preference itself. The distinct parent mode intentionally gives that adaptation judgment to the user/operator. - Evidence: preference-learning.md; personalization.md; profile-observer.mjs; preference-promoter.mjs; session-observer.mjs; module.mjs; frontend-tools.mjs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: autonomous learning is deliberately narrow, optional and limited to four user-trait fields; this state does not claim general self-modification, model training, tool evolution or strategic environmental research.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: maintain the assistant instance&#39;s default identity/persona at the assessed product recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the deployment may require a different stable assistant name, personality, relationship stance or expression style while preserving one coherent instance-wide default across sessions and users&#39; overlays. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: what default assistant identity/personality/relationship stance/expression style this deployed qwen-audio-agent instance presents when not superseded by a legitimate per-user personalization override. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: the deployment user/operator controlling the local ASSISTANT.md (or configured assistant-profile path) is the parent authority for this identity surface. No autonomous first-party S5 owner is claimed. - Return-to-operation path: first launch materializes the packaged assistant-profile template; the parent edits the local profile; qwen-audio-agent preserves it across upgrades and loads it into &lt;assistantprofile&gt; for later voice sessions; the first-party instruction hierarchy then makes that profile govern default identity/persona while keeping core tool/permission/safety/task policy non-overridable by persona or memory. - Disturbance / variety regulated: identity/persona changes are separated from per-user memory and from core operational policy so a deployment-level identity decision can persist without being confused with task permissions or transient conversation state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the instance-wide default assistant identity/personality/relationship/expression profile that subsequent sessions receive. - Decision owner: parent deployment user/operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: local ASSISTANT.md, configurable profile path, template-on-first-launch behavior, upgrade preservation, resolveAssistantProfile/frontend context composition and prompt authority hierarchy. - Closure path: identity/persona decision → parent edits supported local profile → runtime reloads profile on subsequent session → Realtime frontend receives it as authoritative persona-default context → subsequent behaviour reflects returned identity decision. - Boundary reachability: ASSISTANT.md is a documented standard local configuration surface at the frozen revision, not a repository-maintainer/development-only file; the normal frontend instruction builder loads the resolved assistant profile into operating sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime and model consume/enforce the selected profile but do not hold the ultimate right to change the instance-wide profile through the memory/learning path. Documentation explicitly prevents memory from editing ASSISTANT.md; the decisive identity choice remains with the parent operator. - Evidence: personalization.md; deep-dive.md; frontend-tools.mjs; PROMPT.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this S5 credit is intentionally limited to instance identity/persona. Ordinary backend permission approval, task cancellation, static core prompt text and per-user preferences are not used as S5 witnesses.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/96935c61990d83cc34b88a8463b57cdb5beb6295/assessments/qwen-audio-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 96935c61990d83cc34b88a8463b57cdb5beb6295\">96935c6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-02T00:10:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/t54-labs/agent-commons\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Commons</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-01\">2026-10-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/t54-labs/agent-commons/tree/6e911236127dc6cf0d231add87e302974151a7f4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 6e911236127dc6cf0d231add87e302974151a7f4\">6e91123 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform repository-facing coding, validation, deployment or other engineering work as an enrolled Commons-enabled agent work cell while keeping its declared task/plan/evidence state coupled to the coordination system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task-specific repository and tool state, implementation choices, test/deployment feedback, changing local evidence, blockers and responses from the human or peer agents that affect the cell&#39;s next operational action. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose and revise the next task-specific coding/tool/action step from the current objective and returned evidence while respecting Commons coordination constraints. - Decision owner: the supported autonomous coding-agent session (for example Codex, Claude Code or Cline) operating with the shipped Commons Skill in an enrolled workspace. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Commons scope and registration, task/plan records, inbox/messages, lease state, wrappers, durable audit events, local/Relay persistence and the Skill&#39;s lifecycle instructions. - Closure path: enrolled session resolves identity/scope → agent declares task and plan → agent performs task-specific work through its runtime/environment subject to coordination state → repository/tool/environment evidence returns → the same agent revises, continues, completes or escalates and updates Commons state. - Boundary reachability: the verified package carries the canonical Skill and commons install-skill installs it into supported Codex/Claude Code/Cline Skill locations; the shipped Skill then governs enrolled session startup, work and completion without application-authored glue. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the autonomous coding-agent actor while retaining Commons tasks, messages, SQLite, leases and Console leaves coordination infrastructure but no entity that makes the open-ended repository-facing operational decisions. Commons does not itself run the model, so the S1 decision right remains with the participating autonomous agent cell rather than with the deterministic control plane. - Evidence: README.md; .agents/skills/commons/SKILL.md; docs/architecture.md; docs/commons-implementation-status.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the positive state applies to the Commons-enabled assembled operating mode. It does not import planning, delegation, memory, policy or audit functions from the external agent runtime itself, and a workspace enrolled as disabled is outside the active operational organization.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference among independently operating agent cells that intend to mutate the same shared engineering resource, and feed the conflict result back into their subsequent behaviour. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous deploys, migrations, Git writes, browser-profile takeover, server/port use or other overlapping mutations where two agent cells could otherwise act on one shared mutable resource without knowing the other&#39;s current claim. - Distinct S1 units: two or more independently started Commons-enabled coding-agent sessions in the same enrolled local/project scope, potentially across different runtimes, repositories or machines. - Inter-S1 disturbance: a concrete shared-resource collision, such as one agent deploying while another begins a staging smoke/migration operation, or multiple agents attempting writes against the same database, branch, browser profile, server or deployment slot. - Attenuating coordination relation: agents publish plans and inspect peer/lease state; write-like/exclusive/maintenance leases provide canonical resource identity, atomic grant/deny, TTL and fencing epochs; direct messages support holder negotiation and handoff; optional wrappers gate Commons-controlled risky commands against the current lease. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a denied lease is returned to the requesting agent; the shipped Skill says not to proceed and instead contact the holder, wait, change the plan or ask the user. Release/handoff or a new fenced grant then permits later operation, so the coordination result materially changes subsequent S1 action. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the witness is not merely messages or a shared task board. Commons identifies named classes of real shared-resource interference and supplies a conflict relation specifically designed to prevent stale or simultaneous mutation, plus an explicit behavioral return from grant/denial into later agent action. - Decisive decision or feedback right: after Commons exposes the conflict/grant state, the autonomous requesting/holding agents own the discretionary mutual-adjustment response—whether to hand off, wait, revise resource use or escalate—while the deterministic lease engine enforces atomic incompatibility and fencing. - Decision owner: participating autonomous agent cells using the first-party installed Skill; Commons runtime machinery owns grant/deny/fencing enforcement but not the discretionary coordination choice following that feedback. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Relay/local SQLite transactions, canonical resource IDs, lease modes, TTLs, fencing epochs, persisted denial events, heartbeats, task/plan state, direct/broadcast messaging and high-risk command wrappers. - Closure path: agents declare intended shared-resource use → Commons detects/serializes incompatible lease claims → grant or denial is returned → agent adjusts plan/communication/action → holder releases/renews or requester waits/changes course → subsequent resource use reflects the coordinated result. - Boundary reachability: lease acquisition and conflict handling are mandatory paths in the shipped Skill for enrolled sessions; the current implementation includes real local/Relay lease engines and wrappers, so no developer-authored coordinator is required to close the supported agent feedback loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the deterministic lease service would still block incompatible grants if the agent were removed, but it would not choose the organizational response to the conflict or perform a peer handoff/replan. The standard Skill supplies the feedback to an already participating autonomous agent, which owns that mutual-adjustment discretion. The runtime enforcement therefore supports rather than replaces agent ownership. - Evidence: README.md; .agents/skills/commons/SKILL.md; docs/architecture.md; docs/why-commons.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Commons cannot prevent an unrelated process from bypassing a lease unless the downstream system uses a Commons wrapper/hook/fencing check. The S2 claim is therefore about the supported Commons-enabled agent organization, not universal exclusion of all out-of-band actors.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party whole-project current-control function is established at the reviewed boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential project-wide contention among commitments, priorities, resources and exceptions was checked, but the shipped product does not assign a metasystemic actor authority to resolve that variety on behalf of the whole. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established beyond peer/local task decisions, deterministic resource-conflict enforcement and isolated operator interventions. - Decision owner: none established for S3 at the project recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task ownership/status, agent presence, lease state, policy/wrapper checks, audit history, operator-readable Console and CLI mutation primitives. - Closure path: no project-wide S3 decision-and-return loop is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: participating agents regulate their own tasks and negotiate shared-resource conflicts, but no agent is granted whole-project authority over current priorities, commitments or resource allocation. Deterministic leases are S2 conflict attenuation rather than S3 discretion. - Evidence: docs/architecture.md; docs/commons-product-design.md; docs/commons-implementation-status.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a team can use Commons data as input to human management or build future admin policy, but those adjacent arrangements are not a shipped S3 closure at the frozen ref.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary audit path is established inside the supported Commons operating organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertainty about whether agent-reported completion/evidence reflects operational reality was inspected. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none supplied by an independent first-party auditor whose finding returns into project control. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: append-only audit events, hash-chain verification, evidence-bearing task updates, artifacts, message receipts, test/runtime-smoke infrastructure and Skill guidance to verify peer claims against primary evidence. - Closure path: no independent audit judgment → control-return path is supplied by Commons itself. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the ordinary participating agent can inspect repository state or evidence, but that is not materially independent access beyond the operational path. The audit log records what Commons observed; it does not independently challenge an S1 claim. - Evidence: README.md; docs/why-commons.md; docs/commons-implementation-status.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external Git review, tests or an independently run reviewer can supply complementary evidence to a team using Commons, but Commons explicitly says it does not make unverified claims trustworthy and does not package that external acceptance actor as an S3 owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party external-and-prospective adaptation function is established for the Commons organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: possible changes in agent runtimes, team environment, infrastructure, policy and future coordination needs were inspected. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established that converts sensed external/future distinctions into adaptation options and returns them to current capability. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: current peer/resource discovery, heartbeat/activity state, scope resolution, audit history, repository roadmap/product documents and runtime compatibility work. - Closure path: no supported outside-and-then adaptation loop is present. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: participating agents react to current task/resource conditions; that is present-time operational/S2 regulation, not prospective modelling of the environment and adaptation of Commons capability. - Evidence: README.md; docs/architecture.md; docs/commons-implementation-status.md. - Basis: structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: maintainers have roadmap and compatibility work, but those development/governance surfaces are adjacent to the assessed deployed product organization and are not borrowed as runtime S4 ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: close the parent-governed membership/privacy boundary that determines whether a workspace participates in Commons at all and, if so, whether it belongs to local-only or private-Relay coordination. - Disturbance / variety regulated: accidental or unauthorized enrollment of personal/client/otherwise unrelated workspaces into a coordination network; disclosure of coordination metadata to the wrong scope; ambiguous human attribution for newly registered agents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose remote, local or disabled when workspace scope is unknown, and explicitly establish the human owner before an agent can register in an enabled scope. - Decision owner: the legitimate parent human/operator for the workspace. The Skill explicitly forbids inferring the choice or owner and requires an explicit answer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: commons scope resolve, scope enroll, persisted workspace rules/project config, human-owner storage, Relay/project identity, registration checks and the Skill&#39;s prohibition on register/broadcast/inbox/lease activity when scope is disabled or unresolved. - Closure path: session encounters unknown scope → Skill stops and asks parent human → human makes the authoritative participation/scope decision → Commons persists remote, local or disabled enrollment (and human attribution when enabled) → subsequent sessions resolve that decision before registration → enabled sessions join only the selected scope, while disabled sessions perform no Commons coordination operations. - Boundary reachability: scope-first resolution is mandatory in the shipped installed Skill and implemented CLI; it is not a development-only convention or an application-defined callback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the autonomous agent is deliberately denied the ultimate participation choice when scope is unknown. It cannot infer consent or human identity and therefore cannot close this S5 decision internally. The parent decision is returned into runtime behaviour through persisted scope resolution, so the positive mode is P, not a generic human approval annotation. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: whether this workspace is a member of the Commons coordination organization and which privacy/trust boundary governs its coordination metadata and peer visibility. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: parent human/operator only; no first-party autonomous S5 mode is claimed. - Return-to-operation path: persisted scope and human attribution are resolved before each new participating session; they govern whether registration and all subsequent Commons coordination operations are allowed and which local/project boundary they use. - Evidence: .agents/skills/commons/SKILL.md; docs/why-commons.md; docs/architecture.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this S5 mapping is boundary-relative. It treats participation/privacy scope as an identity-level membership decision for the selected Commons project/workspace recursion; ordinary lease approval, task status, token possession or resource policy is not promoted to S5.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4f01503055ce286393b5e66f5a3916732a6dc0c0/assessments/commons.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4f01503055ce286393b5e66f5a3916732a6dc0c0\">4f01503 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-19T20:12:16+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Birfy/agentdescent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentDescent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-19\">2026-10-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Birfy/agentdescent/tree/d1cfa8f6eb570d927826278c70aa23b86d3c5264\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d1cfa8f6eb570d927826278c70aa23b86d3c5264\">d1cfa8f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce model-selected responses to heterogeneous tasks using the current candidate reusable skill/prompt artifact, and obtain outcome feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different task prompts and expected outputs, current skill weaknesses, uncertain model responses and live per-task result variance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The LLMAgent.solve model, reached by buildengine and eng.executor.rollout, produces a contextual answer from the rendered strategy and task; the scorer observes that answer and the same run may then change its candidate strategy through a separate reflection path. - Decision owner: Configured model through shipped LLMAgent and Completion adapter; the harness supplies the call route and feedback, whereas a test/demo&#39;s scripted offlineagent is not proof of model inference. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task and input formatting, scorer, workers/parallel task sampling, executor, candidate render, provider request/usage adapter and run/result logs. - Closure path: Active artifact snapshot + selected task → LLMAgent.solve complete → task response → scored outcome / recorded trajectory → subsequent task operation and separate prospective proposal if wrong. - Boundary reachability: The package exports LLMAgent and evolve, buildengine binds agent.solve to the rollout executor, and public documented examples use run=... / propose=reflector(model); the decisive modeled act is supported in shipped first-party composition, even though the selected provider is external. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model from the supported LLMAgent path leaves sampling, persistence and reward arithmetic but not contextual task-response synthesis. The model&#39;s answer is the S1 decision, not the merge gate&#39;s numerical acceptance. - Evidence: agentdescent/evolution.py#L120, agentdescent/evolution.py#L2250, agentdescent/evolution.py#L3740, agentdescent/agents.py#L1, README.md#L120. - Basis: Source-wired packaged model-backed operating path; no empirical inference performance attested. - Confidence: High for structural wiring, moderate for ability to run under the isolated evidence environment. - Caveats: The underlying task-solving model may itself be an application-supplied tool-using agent; its organizational metasystem and separate task services are not imported into AgentDescent.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No positively evidenced organizational inter-S1 interference regulator with an autonomous ownership path and subsequent coordinated adjustments between independently viable S1 operating divisions is shown at this recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent rollout workers may propose contradictory edits against shared keys, race against stale ledger snapshots or exceed their task slice; these disturbances are candidate-artifact merge conflicts, not evidenced competing independent S1 divisional commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Default DefaultConflict.resolve chooses higher held-out-scoring contradicting diffs and DefaultFusion combines compatible ops; optional ReflectiveFusion can use a model to synthesize contested values. These are change-merger optimization decisions for one shared artifact, not separate mutual regulation of independently viable service departments. - Decision owner: Deterministic default conflict/verifier policy (or optionally a model behind pluggable fusion) governs candidate admission; no first-party organization-level S2 owner is demonstrated for independently operating S1 units. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-worker snapshots, staleness filters, evidence buffer, DefaultConflict, DefaultFusion, optional ReflectiveFusion, section guards and serialized single-merger commit. - Closure path: Candidate Diff cards → conflict/merge rule → held-out gate → updated single artifact head → next worker snapshot; this is coherence of an experimental artifact, not sourced S1-vs-S1 interference/oscillation → S2 damping → coordinated separate S1 behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Even model-assisted diff synthesis is not automatically an organizational S2 function; removing it leaves the same underlying single-artifact optimization goal rather than an autonomous coordinating relationship among distinct S1 departments. - Evidence: agentdescent/defaults.py#L90, agentdescent/defaults.py#L141, agentdescent/fusion.py#L127, agentdescent/aggregator.py#L827, agentdescent/evolution.py#L3686. - Basis: Bounded negative after checking both default and optional model-merger paths. - Confidence: Medium-high at the declared learning-harness recursion. - Caveats: If this framework is used to optimize several independently viable agents sharing operational resources, a new application-specific assessment boundary might expose real S2 evidence; generic N worker count does not prove it.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No autonomous executive management of a portfolio of independently viable present S1 operations with negotiated priorities/resources and binding feedback is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Worker errors, budgets, round timeouts, task allocation and token spending can destabilize an evolution run, but the linked responses are configured limits/scheduling and local algorithmic optimization, not enterprise-level management. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Caller sets workers, evaluation threshold, compute limits, task selection/policy and trust region. BudgetGovernor and worker health enforce/calculate thresholds; aggregator determines candidate fitness, not organizational present obligations across S1 divisions. - Decision owner: Operator configuration and deterministic package code; specific S1 model answers and S4 reflection are separate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: BudgetGovernor, sampler, WorkerHealth, max-worker-errors, timeout, token/governance/trust regions and commit policy. - Closure path: Resource/error readings → fixed or configured scheduling/budget/sampler actions → continue/stop/skip individual experiment; no first-party manager discretion over cross-S1 present capabilities and reallocation with an organizational return path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Adaptive numeric compute placement and candidate selection are not a discretionary S3 authority over S1 commitments. - Evidence: agentdescent/evolution.py#L2795, agentdescent/evolution.py#L3515, agentdescent/budget.py#L1, agentdescent/pipeline.py#L49, agentdescent/aggregator.py#L1412. - Basis: Source-bounded negative across current-control and mode variants. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The human operator of a portfolio of deployment agents may be a real S3 externally; it is not a packaged parent-governed return path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary audit of S1/S3 operational reality with a mandate to correct a current S3 managerial picture and a returned management decision path is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model-generated candidate edits could corrupt performance, hallucinate improvements or violate artifact invariants; the held-out/oracle check independently measures candidate success, but is principally a change-acceptance/quality gate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The ThreeLayerVerifier and AuditScheduler supply evidence and can veto/queue validation for prospective artifact changes. They do not inspect competing operational reports against underlying reality and return corrective findings to an established S3 owner in the assessed boundary. - Decision owner: Deterministic verifier/audit thresholds and external supplied reward/oracle; no separate first-party complementary auditor with governance corrective mandate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Held-out rewards, oracle audit queue, replay/diagnostics, immutable L0 guards, statistical selection and change acceptance. - Closure path: Candidate rollout evidence → held-out verifier/audit/acceptance decision → changed or refused future artifact; no independent operational inspection → correction of S3 current enterprise control → management action. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The audit terminology describes robust scientific evaluation for future learning, not necessarily VSM S3; model/reflection and a scorer can occupy S1/S4 feedback without a distinct operational executive audit relationship. - Evidence: agentdescent/verifier.py#L1, agentdescent/aggregator.py#L968, agentdescent/audit/wiring.py#L1, agentdescent/governance.py#L70. - Basis: Explicit audit/evaluation mechanics assessed under their concrete organizational closure role. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A deployment could adopt independent run-time compliance audits as an application function, requiring its own evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Learn from environment/task performance and explore future changes to the task-serving skill/prompt/harness, then adopt and feed those changes into the next operational version. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future task mix, repeated solver failure modes, reward changes, competition between candidate strategies and uncertainty about which generalized edits will improve upcoming task success. - External distinction: The worker runs the current strategy on sampled environmental tasks, records model outputs, measured reward and expected-result metadata, and uses those observations as evidence of current strategy inadequacy. - Future / prospective distinction: The LLMAgent.propose / reflector(complete) model reads the failed task, current rules, output and reward and proposes a generalized edit to the persistent future reusable artifact, not just a next-turn reply to the same task. - Adaptation option generated: The model chooses a candidate new instruction/rule/skill/harness value; Strategy.todiff produces a concrete prospective Diff, possibly alongside alternative proposals generated by peer workers. - Path back into current capability / S3: The aggregator scores/filters candidates with held-out evidence and invariant/governance constraints, atomically commits accepted Diff to the versioned ledger and the next rollout snapshots read the updated artifact. The return is into the operating solver&#39;s future capability, not into an independently evidenced S3 executive; absence of an S3 owner is not hidden. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Prospective option formulation belongs to the configured model through the shipped reflection adapter. The commit/adoption veto belongs to deterministic held-out/invariant gates and ultimately the operator-selected reward/constraints, which bound (but do not replace) model-generated strategic content. - Decision owner: Model-backed LLMAgent.propose or reflector in the supported shipped first-party evolve mode; deterministic gates authorize materialization, not conceptual option invention. Autonomous S4 is bounded to proposing and adopting prospective improvements under a predefined learning mission, not constitutional policy authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: EvidenceCard, snapshot/ledger, selection and staleness, optional reflective model fusion, ThreeLayerVerifier, reward gate, budgets, stop conditions and transaction logs. - Closure path: External task-result contrast → model reflection/options → diff → held-out/invariant acceptance → committed persistent strategy/skill version → subsequent task solver uses new artifact → fresh feedback round. - Boundary reachability: LLMAgent.propose and public reflector(model) are shipped, buildengine actually wires them into evolve, proposeone calls propose(mine.render(),task,output,score), aggregator ingests card and commits to the same ledger used for next worker snapshots; public quickstart exercises this integrated model-and-artifact mode. The offline demo runs the same algorithm with a scripted surrogate, not claimed as model evidence. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Remove the proposer model while leaving worker scheduler, scoring, merge and ledger intact and the general semantic rule/strategy revision would not be generated from task evidence; fixed gates can accept/refuse candidates but cannot themselves invent the task-specific future improvement. - Evidence: agentdescent/evolution.py#L120, agentdescent/evolution.py#L206, agentdescent/evolution.py#L2250, agentdescent/evolution.py#L3740, agentdescent/evolution.py#L3790, agentdescent/aggregator.py#L1412, agentdescent/ledger.py#L1. - Basis: Source-connected first-party LLM reflection and durable future version adoption; not evidence of production task-success gains. - Confidence: High for the option→accept→next-run wiring, medium for realized reward improvement. - Caveats: This is a bounded future operating-capability adaptation path; it does not imply independent monitoring of society, a full organizational S3/S5, constitutional delegation, or automatic improvement without configured model/reward/dataset.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No legitimate first-party agent/human constitutional authority is wired to receive fundamental purpose/identity conflicts and return binding enduring identity policy for the assessed organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Dangerous self-modification and oracle/policy corruption are anticipated through frozen L0 and invariant guards; these are static protections rather than governance of identity conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator/developer sets the objective, evaluator, immutable artifact ids, blast radius and thresholds. assertmutable rejects frozen IDs and fixed policies enforce configured criteria; no bounded protocol resolves ultimate-purpose questions through a legitimate S5 authority. - Decision owner: External task/operator for constitutional objectives, deterministic gate for enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: L0 frozen IDs, governance classify/immutable assertions, contract invariants, rewards, public configurable policies and audit gates. - Closure path: Configured constraint → proposed invalid mutation refused → continue or error; no question of organizational identity reaches a first-party S5 actor and returns as future binding identity policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Preventing a model from rewriting its own oracle is important security, but a hard-coded immutable condition cannot adjudicate the organization&#39;s ultimate purpose or legitimate authority. - Evidence: agentdescent/governance.py#L70, agentdescent/governance.py#L134, agentdescent/evolution.py#L2270, agentdescent/aggregator.py#L1426. - Basis: Bounded negative from both model-driven and governance paths. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: External human owners may enforce or revise ultimate goals; the packaged learning engine contains no first-party return transaction qualifying as parent-governed S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c33e18ca6ab5f02cf7a5005434c9eefa821c06f7/assessments/ag/agentdescent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c33e18ca6ab5f02cf7a5005434c9eefa821c06f7\">c33e18c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T18:05:09+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/theStrangeAdventurer/capstan\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Capstan</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/theStrangeAdventurer/capstan/tree/60f284fc94ce96d05e72b52d2ffe55b4096b0177\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 60f284fc94ce96d05e72b52d2ffe55b4096b0177\">60f284f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement/inspect/verify a requested change through iterative model-selected actions in the project workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: varying user code requests, repository contents, tool failures, tests, model/tool availability, permission denials and returned outputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select next tool/action, revise approach from tool/test output, request delegation, and determine when the code change is complete. - Decision owner: Capstan&#39;s main model loop; child models own bounded delegated operational tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: C host, provider transport, Lua tool dispatcher, skills/profiles, sandbox and permit controls, sessions, guard and telemetry. - Closure path: prompt → model tool decision → permission-gated first-party action → returned observation → next model decision and correction or final response. - Boundary reachability: the shipped implement and headless/ACP modes call the same live agent runtime and expose the first-party coding tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: C executes and enforces; the model retains the task-specific operational discretion and reacts to evidence. - Evidence: runtime.lua, tools.lua, agent-loop.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external models/providers and approval rules are necessary dependencies/constraints, not additional credited autonomous owners.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no first-party feedback loop specifically attenuating conflict or oscillation BETWEEN distinct S1 work units is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel child tasks may contend for provider slots, tools and a shared workspace, but no demonstrated task-to-task interference regulator with feedback into subsequent individual S1 choices is shipped. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none meeting S2-specific closure at the declared recursion. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child concurrency cap, inherited permission scope, per-child whitelist and turn limits, result ordering, deterministic queue, retry/cancellation. - Closure path: not established as S2; child results are returned to a parent after execution. - Boundary reachability: no positive path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a parent agent delegates tasks, while runtime queue and guard enforce chosen limits; neither demonstrates specific cross-child conflicting-operation detection and attenuation. - Evidence: subagents spec, subagent runtime, task store. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: concurrency-cap resource scheduling can prevent overload, but should not be re-labelled inter-S1 coordination absent disturbance-specific returned regulation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-current-operation management loop with a current comprehensive view, substantive portfolio control and returned supervisory intervention is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: budgets, asynchronous child work and task-plan status are bounded locally; whole-current organization regulation is not demonstrated. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identified management decision over all active S1 commitments beyond planning and routing individual work. - Decision owner: no S3 actor established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: parent subagent orchestration, immutable ordered batch results, timeout/turn caps, local task statuses/revision, headless/TUI run status and cancellation. - Closure path: model sees completed child results and may launch more work; no live whole-current control or feedback path over active commitments is shown. - Boundary reachability: no positive path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model task selection and deterministic scheduling are not by themselves a whole-current authority. Human cancellation/permission popups likewise do not constitute a supervised current-control mode. - Evidence: subagents spec, tools.lua, tasks.lua, agent-control spec. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: genuine autonomous task-level routing is credited to S1, not automatically S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: shipped read-only child-model/tool compositions may furnish direct workspace access for a separate review judgment, but a complete independent complementary-audit/corrective-return path is not yet conclusively evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: implementation mistakes and discrepancies between agent claims and actual files/tests could be examined, but the distinct review authority/return path needs confirmation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: potentially a read-only child-agent with fresh context evaluating artifacts; separate corrective-control ownership and actual triggering remain unresolved. - Decision owner: unresolved; the same-agent completion-review continuation is not independent, while an explicitly prompted subagent might make a first-party complementary audit possible. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: independent child Lua execution context, read-only profile/tools, fileread and shell inspection if allowed, model-selected delegation, and optional same-agent completion-review switch. - Closure path: potential parent initiates independent inspection → receives child findings → decides correction; no concrete built-in verification-trigger and return-to-action workflow was established by inspected sources. - Boundary reachability: a positive state is not claimed; child-agent read access and parent batch-result collection are shipped, but an audit-specific operational mode still requires function-level evidence. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: mere generic delegation or the self-review continuation must not be treated as independent S3. The unresolved question is whether a concrete supported standard-mode parent-to-read-only-child audit has the required complementary authority and feedback. - Evidence: tools.lua, subagents spec, agent profiles, runtime completion-review implementation. - Basis: structural + unknown. - Confidence: low. - Caveats: requires source-first semantic adjudication of direct evidence access, independence boundary and real corrective return; no independently executed reviewer receipt is claimed here.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: in the opt-in shipped self-improvement mode, assess future repeat-use needs and construct a durable local agent capability in response. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new/repeating operator workflows and capability gaps whose solution must persist beyond one coding request. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select reusable command/model tool/hook shape, design a Lua plugin and optional skill, write it with first-party tools, and revise from validation/feedback. - Decision owner: the Capstan model when the owner has enabled the first-party self-improvement capability and authorized the relevant write. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configuration flag, embedded skill loader, ordinary permission checks, plugin scanner and hot reload, durable Lua plugin and skill directories. - Closure path: prospective workflow need detected in the user/environment → model designs persistent capability → permission-gated plugin/skill write → C hot reload registers future executable behavior → subsequent Capstan runs can invoke the new command/tool/hook. - Boundary reachability: the shipped C loader intentionally enables the bundled skill when capabilities.selfimprovement is true; shipped file-write/shell model tools can create the suggested durable plugin, and C watches/reloads it in the supported installation. This is an explicit opt-in first-party mode, not borrowed CI/dogfood or a hypothetical third-party plugin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the human controls whether the mode is enabled and grants file permissions, but the model determines the prospective adaptation content and implements the persistent future capability. The hot-reload loop enforces, rather than chooses, the change. - External distinction: prospective repeated workflow need and owner/project environment not yet served by present built-in command/tool set. - Future / prospective distinction: the requested extension should remain useful in later sessions and may serve later as a command, model tool, or hook. - Adaptation option generated: executable local Lua plugin, optional paired skill and automation hook. - Path back into current capability / S3: newly created plugin is hot-loaded into the active Capstan command/tool/hook registry for later agent use; no positive S3 is inferred. - Evidence: self-improvement skill, self-improvement spec, embedded skill loader, plugin reload. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: disabled by default; first-party agent may suggest an extension but cannot bypass the human&#39;s explicit capability and file permissions. This is not proof that Capstan automatically evolves itself without user-context needs or authorization. Independent semantic review should scrutinize whether the outside-and-then adaptation right is adequately instantiated in this opt-in mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate first-party ultimate organizational identity/purpose arbiter with returned constitutional-policy decision is established within the assessed session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permission exceptions, selected agent profile, provider choice and extension capabilities are operational/configuration constraints, not demonstrated S5 identity reconciliation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for whole-system ultimate purpose/identity; owner configuration and permits set local limits. - Decision owner: no evidenced first-party S5 decision loop; ordinary parent user consent is not enough for a P state. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Lua configuration, profile selection, permit rules, static system prompt, first-party skill enablement and project AGENTS.md. - Closure path: user-selected execution permissions affect tools but no articulated identity/purpose matter traverses a legitimate ultimate-policy body and returns as authoritative organizational policy for future operation. - Boundary reachability: no positive path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves preset policy enforcement, not autonomous ultimate-purpose decisions; removing operator prompts removes configuration without revealing an autonomous S5 owner. - Evidence: permissions, profile policy, system prompt. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: advanced externally authored governance policies are possible but not automatically part of this assessed distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2071d6aa11c9db2bf4b892eef326a369c19479eb/assessments/capstan.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2071d6aa11c9db2bf4b892eef326a369c19479eb\">2071d6a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T22:28:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ccxxxyy/mini-code-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mini-Code-Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ccxxxyy/mini-code-agent/tree/ee8c4339b0dd04088377f0c27e04abc1bf9904b9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ee8c4339b0dd04088377f0c27e04abc1bf9904b9\">ee8c433 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute developer operations using model-decided reading, patching, shell, search, task and optionally delegated child-agent actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing code/user goals, test and tool failures, permission denials, child/peer reports and varying repository state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each AgentLoop selects and revises substantive tool operations from observed results; child models own their bounded local operational decisions. - Decision owner: configured primary model and separately instantiated child models. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry, async streaming, sandbox/permission manager, context compression, memory retrieval, event bus, tool result cache and differentiated child policies. - Closure path: task/context → model-selected tool calls → actual tool result/denial → feedback event/conversation → next model decision or completion. - Boundary reachability: Application constructs the primary loop and tool registry; SubAgentManager instantiates independent AgentLoop children for /spawn, model spawnagents and /team in shipped modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-backed loops select task-specific action and repair, unlike fixed enforcement and scheduling code. - Evidence: AgentLoop, SubAgent, application. - Basis: frozen executable source and mock-driven script tests. - Confidence: high for actual agent operating construction; not a live capability benchmark. - Caveats: provider calls and real model outcomes were not executed; one native subagent test hung under the local runner.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: regulate interference between concurrently deciding read/write child agents and dependent coding steps by model-selected isolated workspace and planned dependency boundaries. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel worker file collisions, premature execution before dependent research is complete and competing modifications of a shared repository tree. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the primary model selects the isolated option and delegated work topology through spawnagents; model-backed Planner selects per-step dependson/writesfiles, while the runtime enforces separate worktrees/read-only tool restrictions and dependency batches. - Decision owner: primary and planning models for coordination choices, deterministic SubAgentManager, AgentTeam and WorktreeManager for execution/enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-child Git worktrees, read-only child tool filtering, independent child task scheduling, dependency result gates and returned child/merge reports. - Closure path: potential overlap/dependency → model chooses isolation/ordered plan → independent S1s execute in bounded trees/batches → output/failure and dependency reports return to parent → subsequent task assignment can change. - Boundary reachability: shipped model tool spawnagents exposes isolated=true, which passes isolation=&#39;worktree&#39; to SubAgentManager; /team creates model-backed Planner plus team batches and passes dependency results to later workers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: selecting isolation and cross-task dependencies is discretionary model judgment, while worktree creation, tool filtering and dependency checks enforce the selected relation. This is optional and not an unconditional guarantee of global conflict-free merging. - Evidence: spawn tool, team, planner, worktree. - Basis: direct frozen coordination paths and isolated mock/team/worktree tests. - Confidence: medium-high for the bounded model-selected coordination choice and enforcement. - Caveats: defaults permit non-isolated children; dependency planning can make poor choices and Git branch integration remains a separate source of unresolved conflicts. - Distinct S1 units: independent model-backed SubAgent/pane workers running separate conversation/tool loops. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping edits in one repository tree and tasks attempted before prerequisites complete. - Attenuating coordination relation: model-selected worktree isolation plus planned dependency batches and automatic removal of write tools from read-only team members. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: child results and prior-step outputs enter the next team prompt or primary spawnagents tool result; interference/error signals can change later work. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: code explicitly changes the file-write collision topology and prevents dependency-violating/write-unauthorized peer execution rather than merely forwarding messages.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: provide the legitimate local operator a whole-current roster of spawned agent commitments and targeted intervention control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stuck, obsolete or competing-priority background children continuing to use finite time and tool/model resources. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an attending user invokes /spawn list, decides which active child should stop, and calls /spawn cancel &lt;id&gt; or cancel-all. - Decision owner: human operator as parent for the current session; fixed SubAgentManager.cancel implements the selected stop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: listactive, getstatus, activesnapshots, current-agent progress board and cancel hooks propagated to child AgentLoop. - Closure path: whole-current list/phase → human chooses specific running agent → /spawn cancel &lt;id&gt; → SubAgentManager.cancel aborts its loop/task → changed active roster/remaining commitments. - Boundary reachability: Application wires the manager into first-party slash commands; /spawn list and /spawn cancel &lt;id&gt; are direct regular installed command handlers, independent of optional external governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the human operator decides a whole-current intervention, while the manager enforces it. The model-facing spawnagents can create children and receive results, but has no equivalent first-party current full-roster/selective-cancel tool; hence P, not A(P). - Evidence: slash commands, manager roster/cancel, progress UI. - Basis: first-party human current roster/action path and deterministic command tests. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded session control. - Caveats: human supervision is optional and requires attention; resource budgeting outside active child roster and independent installations is not proven. - Whole-system current view: /spawn list enumerates every active child id and phase in the current session; the board displays current child snapshots and progress. - Current-control decision scope: operator-selected cancellation of one or all current child agent commitments, changing ongoing work rather than merely gating an isolated file edit.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: provide a separate read-only model-run verifier to inspect a stated condition and issue an independent structured PASS/FAIL verdict. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect or incomplete claims from normal coder output that ordinary model self-reporting or passing task actions can overlook. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an optional verify child independently reads code/runs read-only checks, calls syntheticoutput with verdict/failures and returns its report; the parent/operator is free to act on it. - Decision owner: separately instantiated verification model for its judgment; user/parent for optional invocation and follow-through. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: distinct verify type prompt, restricted read-only tool list, isolated child conversation, syntheticoutput extraction and SubAgentResult.structuredoutput. - Closure path: user/parent selects verify child → separate source inspection and structured verdict → returned child output → possible parent/user correction. No mandatory or universally automatic correction gate is enforced. - Boundary reachability: agenttype=&#39;verify&#39; is a shipped spawnagents input and /spawn --type verify command option; SubAgent selects getagenttype policy/tools and extracts the structured result from its own transcript. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the auditor&#39;s model owns a distinct evidence-based verdict rather than merely formatting the coder&#39;s report, but independent audit→enforced current-control corrective closure needs explicit composition; therefore C rather than A. - Evidence: types/verify prompt, child tool/structured output, spawn tool. - Basis: directly exposed first-party verifier constructor, source-enforced tool restriction and offline type tests. - Confidence: medium for meaningful optional independent review construction, not reviewed model quality. - Caveats: reviewer is not necessarily institutionally/provider independent; a user-defined code-review skill or hash-chain audit log alone is not promoted to S3 and no mandatory audit-controlled repair was observed. - Claim being audited: condition or correctness claim about the working project and its implementation. - Ordinary reporting path: primary worker&#39;s own tool observations, edits/tests and completion summary. - Complementary access path: a fresh child conversation with read-only code/tool access and a verdict-specific synthetic output instruction. - Independence boundary: distinct child inference/context and enforced read-only policy; no organizational independence or separate provider required. - Who acts on findings: parent/model or human may initiate repair after receiving the returned report; no first-party mandatory veto/correction obligation is established.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no complete agent-owned prospective external sensing, option development and adopted future-capability return loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future tool/model/skill changes could require adaptation, but current mechanisms mainly provide session context, user configuration and task-specific retrieval. - Decisive decision or feedback right: main agent can invoke installskill for a supplied local/Git source, but no shipped autonomous prospective adaptation choice or external/future scan is connected to this as an organizational loop. - Decision owner: operator or present-task model for explicit skill install and project preferences, fixed memory extraction/consolidation for session context retention. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MemoryExtractor, MemoryConsolidator, skill registry, installskill, plugin loading and /skill commands. - Closure path: user/agent may install an existing skill or extract explicit user preferences into memory; no outside-and-future distinction → adaptation alternatives → first-party adoption judgment → changed continuing capability circuit is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: automatic extraction of user-stated preferences is not future capability selection; a generic model-callable skill installer does not alone own the prospective judgment about which external capabilities the organization should acquire. - Evidence: memory extractor, consolidation, skill install, skills. - Basis: inspected first-party durable-memory/skill paths and their authority boundary. - Confidence: medium for bounded negative; a stronger shipped prospective policy could change classification at a newer pinned ref. - Caveats: skill installation materially changes available runbooks, but source acceptance and requested installation alone are not sufficient evidence for a complete adaptive S4 owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate ultimate identity/policy decision institution and returned governing loop is established for a local Mini-Code-Agent deployment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ultimate purpose and legitimate cross-objective tradeoffs remain operator/developer choices external to the shipped current runtime governance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator sets tactical permissions/model modes and developer rules; no separate highest-policy authority decides identity-level issues and governs later lower-system choices. - Decision owner: user for local tool permissions/identity hints and upstream maintainers for software design, not an installed S5 body. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PermissionManager, mode rules, OS sandbox, sensitive path restrictions, append-only hash-chain action audit and prompt instructions. - Closure path: action request → allow/ask/deny or audit record → task proceeds/fails; no identity-level policy issue → ultimate authority → return affecting organizational purpose. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: recording tamper-evident tool actions or checking file permission is accountability/enforcement infrastructure, not deciding what the organization is ultimately for. - Evidence: permissions, audit log, app wiring. - Basis: policy/identity and audit runtime paths inspected. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the operator legitimately governs local work, but shipped controls do not form S5=P without the ultimate-policy loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c02cac9aa931535a6470913b0fd45adb1c3578cf/assessments/mi/mini-code-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c02cac9aa931535a6470913b0fd45adb1c3578cf\">c02cac9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-22T21:07:39+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/risa-labs-inc/BossConsole\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BossConsole</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-22\">2026-09-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/risa-labs-inc/BossConsole/tree/d4db04882425327915e0520a366eac2da8af01f3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d4db04882425327915e0520a366eac2da8af01f3\">d4db048 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform bounded software-engineering and other tool-mediated work in the relevant project/environment through an attached external AI coding-agent session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user objectives, project/repository state, terminal/browser/file feedback, tool results, model uncertainty, external services and task-local failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose reasoning, tool invocations and concrete task actions within the admitted objective and BOSS-enforced authority envelope. - Decision owner: the autonomous Claude Code, Codex, Gemini or OpenCode/model actor attached through the first-party BOSS terminal/MCP path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: BossTerm, agent attachment/re-attachment, MCP registry, terminal/browser/editor/file/git tools, secrets, automation, RBAC/policy/approval and per-tool kill switches. - Closure path: objective is given to an attached coding agent → the agent reasons and chooses BOSS/MCP actions → BOSS transports/enforces those calls → project/tool/environment feedback returns to the agent → the agent continues until the bounded outcome is produced. - Boundary reachability: the pinned README documents launching the supported CLIs in BOSS terminals, attaching them to the local BOSS MCP server and automatically re-attaching them across restarts/port changes; this is a normal installed operating path rather than repository CI or dogfood infrastructure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: BOSS constrains and enables the work, but the substantive local task decisions remain with the external autonomous agent. Policy enforcement does not transfer S1 ownership to the deterministic host. - Evidence: README.md; docs/CLI.md; composeApp/src/commonMain/kotlin/ai/rever/boss/mcp/WorkspaceMcpToolProvider.kt. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the external agent runtime is credited only as the autonomous actor reached through BOSS&#39;s standard distribution; its internal harness functions are not inherited into BossConsole.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent destructive interference when multiple sibling coding-agent evolution sessions work on different changes to the same plugin repository in parallel. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two or more evolution agents operating in one source checkout can overwrite or mix one another&#39;s edits/branch state while independently changing the same plugin. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the isolation/coordination relation under which concurrent evolution work is separated into dedicated worktrees/branches rather than sharing one checkout. - Decision owner: the reviewed standard distribution does not package an autonomous S2 coordinator for this choice. The first-party Tool Evolver exposes an S2-specific worktree mode and allocator; an autonomous owner would still have to be composed over that path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: EvolveMode.WORKTREE, ensureWorktree, dedicated evolve/&lt;slug&gt; branches, separate working directories, tracked evolution sessions and branch-specific generated skill guidance. - Closure path: several bounded evolution sessions target the same plugin → Worktree mode establishes a dedicated &lt;repo&gt;/.worktrees/&lt;slug&gt; checkout and evolve/&lt;slug&gt; branch per change → the selected CLI agent is launched with that isolated directory/branch → its subsequent file/git behaviour occurs in the separated worktree instead of clobbering sibling work. - Boundary reachability: the worktree mode is a shipped Tool Evolver UI/runtime path in the first-party companion explicitly integrated by BOSS; it is not a CI-only repository convention. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime mechanically creates and enforces the chosen worktree relation, but no autonomous actor in the standard path owns the decision to select that coordination mode. The primitive is nevertheless S2-specific because its own documentation states that it exists so several feature/issue evolutions for the same plugin can run in parallel without clobbering each other. - Evidence: EvolveLauncher.kt; EvolverTabViewModel.kt. - Basis: structural + explicit intent comment. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic JVM multithreading, multiple terminals, multiple attached agents and workspace plurality are not credited as S2. The positive mapping is limited to the concrete same-plugin parallel-evolution interference witness and its dedicated worktree relation. - Distinct S1 units: separate external coding-agent evolution sessions launched in separate BossTerm tabs, each responsible for a bounded feature/issue change to the same plugin. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent sessions sharing one plugin checkout can clobber one another&#39;s source/git state; EvolveLauncher explicitly defines worktree mode so several features/issues can be evolved in parallel without clobbering each other. - Attenuating coordination relation: one dedicated git worktree and evolve/&lt;slug&gt; branch per evolution session/change. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the selected coding agent is launched with the isolated worktree as its working directory and receives branch guidance requiring commits/PR work on that dedicated branch. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the relation is tied to a named concrete interference mode between sibling operational agents over the same mutable repository, and it changes their subsequent filesystem/git context specifically to prevent that interference.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: regulate the running BOSS installation as a whole by observing current workspace/tool/plugin health and deciding which current workspaces, terminals, capabilities and agent tool rights remain active. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current workspaces can be active/stopped, plugins can fail or become unhealthy, MCP calls can be denied/failed, and tool access or current agent resource use may need intervention on behalf of the whole installation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose current interventions such as opening/closing workspaces or terminals, enabling/disabling agent tools, setting current MCP policy/approval decisions, and acting on installation health/recovery findings. - Decision owner: base constructor mode — BOSS intentionally exposes structured whole-installation state and current-control operations to AI/automation clients, but does not package a distinct autonomous whole-system controller that owns those choices. Parent mode — the BOSS operator using the shipped workspace, Toolbox/MCP policy, kill-switch and health/recovery controls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: boss status --json, boss doctor, MCP registry/list/describe/invoke, WorkspaceMcpToolProvider, policy engine, approval UI, per-tool kill switch, recent MCP activity ledger, plugin watchdog and health/recovery surfaces. - Closure path: current installation/workspace/tool health and activity is observed → controller/operator chooses a present intervention → BOSS workspace/MCP/policy/plugin machinery applies it → exposed tools/workspaces/terminals or plugin state change → subsequent agent operation proceeds under the revised current state. - Boundary reachability: the CLI agent harness and workspace lifecycle MCP provider are packaged runtime interfaces; operator MCP policy/kill-switch and recovery controls are normal desktop surfaces. No maintainer or repository-development action is required for the claimed current-control paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: BOSS exposes enough function-specific state and authority for an autonomous controller to be composed, but ordinary attached coding agents are not documented as the installation&#39;s whole-system S3 owner. Deterministic policy/watchdog/lifecycle machinery enforces decisions rather than supplying that discretion. The operator mode does close the S3 decision and return path. - Evidence: docs/CLI.md; WorkspaceMcpToolProvider.kt; README.md; docs/release-notes/v9.5.19.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: individual approvals, process lifecycle, health checks and tool toggles are not promoted separately to S3; the positive mapping relies on their combination with installation-wide/current workspace/tool state and authority to change subsequent operation. - Whole-system current view: boss status --json/boss doctor report the running instance, active project and cross-area plugin/browser/MCP health; listworkspaces reports all existing workspaces with active/running status; the MCP registry and recent activity view expose the current governed tool plane and recent policy outcomes. - Current-control decision scope: open/create/close workspaces and terminals, change which tools an agent may call through policy/kill switches, and perform current plugin recovery/intervention based on health findings.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary organizational audit path is established for independently challenging an S1 producer&#39;s operational claim and returning findings into current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: BOSS exposes health checks, activity/audit records, plugin signatures, security validation and Tool Evolver probe/verification, but these surfaces do not by themselves create an independent organizational challenge to ordinary S1 reporting. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an independent audit judgment over an S1 claim. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: boss doctor, health/watchdog reporting, MCP operation/activity ledgers, signature/integrity checks, policy validation, Tool Evolver memory/log probes and the evolve skill&#39;s self-verification step were inspected but are not credited as S3 ownership. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewed runtime supplies telemetry, deterministic integrity/security checks and producer-controlled/self-invoked verification, but no separate autonomous auditor with materially complementary access whose adverse finding is returned into current control. - Evidence: docs/CLI.md; README.md; evolve-skill-body.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: repository CI/Claude review workflows are adjacent development organization and excluded from the installed harness boundary. Tool Evolver verification is called by the same adaptation agent loop and therefore does not supply organizational independence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: adapt the BOSS tool/capability repertoire when current or anticipated user/task/environment requirements expose a capability change worth implementing. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing project/user requirements and tool behaviour can make an installed capability insufficient, faulty or suboptimal; the system must develop and test a revised capability for subsequent work without taking the running harness offline. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which installed plugin/capability should be evolved and what adaptation request should be pursued, then accept the resulting live-loaded change for continued use in the running BOSS instance. - Decision owner: autonomous base mode — an authorized attached agent can inspect available tools/probe evidence and invoke the first-party evolverevolve MCP tool itself with a selected plugin, agent and optional evolution task; the spawned coding agent owns implementation choices and applies/verifies the changed capability through evolverhotreload. Parent mode — the human operator selects the plugin, requested change and evolve action through the first-party Tool Evolver UI. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: evolverlisttools, evolverprobe, permission-gated evolverevolve, source-repository discovery/clone, generated evolve skill, build tooling, evolverhotreload, live plugin loader, probe/log verification, evolution session tracking and PR creation. - Closure path: external/user/task requirement or observed capability shortfall reaches an authorized agent/operator → a plugin and evolution task are selected → Tool Evolver launches a coding agent against the plugin source → the agent implements/builds → the resulting jar is hot-reloaded into the running BOSS instance → evolverprobe/logs verify the live capability and the agent iterates as needed → subsequent BOSS operation uses the changed tool; a PR is then opened for upstream persistence. - Boundary reachability: the pinned host README advertises Tool Evolver as a supported first-party Toolbox component and explicitly says an agent can reshape a tool while the app remains running; the pinned host contains the runtime Evolver MCP contract, and the contemporaneous first-party plugin exposes the agent-facing evolve/hot-reload tools used by that shipped path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: unlike generic plugin hot-reload or self-improvement naming, the base mode gives an autonomous agent the actual adaptation decision/action path: select a target and task, launch evolution, implement, live-load, observe and iterate. Deterministic loader/build machinery supports that decision. A separate operator mode lets the parent own the adaptation request instead. - Evidence: README.md; EvolverContract.kt; Tool Evolver README; ToolEvolverMcpTools.kt; evolve-skill-body.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: internal memory/leak/log probing alone would not establish S4. The positive finding depends on the complete future-capability loop and on an external/user/task distinction supplying the adaptation need. The A claim is bounded to runtime capability of the installed BOSS instance; opening a PR does not give the agent ultimate authority over upstream maintainer merge/release governance. - External distinction: the adaptation request arises from user/project/task requirements in BOSS&#39;s environment, and Tool Evolver can acquire the target plugin&#39;s external source repository; the system is not merely consolidating internal memory or replaying a prior run. - Future / prospective distinction: the requested evolution is explicitly aimed at changing the tool so subsequent agent work has a different capability; the evolve loop tests the revised plugin live before upstream persistence. - Adaptation option generated: an authorized agent/operator selects a target and evolution task; Tool Evolver launches an AI coding agent that develops a concrete plugin modification on an evolution branch/worktree. - Path back into current capability / S3: evolverhotreload copies the newly built jar into the running host&#39;s plugin directory, unloads/loads it without restart, and the evolve skill probes/verifies the live result before subsequent operation continues.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure path is established at the running BOSS recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: BOSS has extensive RBAC, per-tool policy, approvals, secrets, role/admin semantics, plugin signing and capability-evolution controls, but these regulate operational authority, current control, security and adaptation rather than deciding what BOSS ultimately is or which identity-defining policy prevails. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an identity/ultimate-policy issue. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: server-side RBAC/RLS, admin permission semantics, MCP allow/ask/deny policy, per-tool kill switch, secrets, signed plugins, operator approvals, plugin install/update/evolution and external upstream PR governance were inspected but are not promoted to S5. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: there is no established S5 function to assign. Operator supremacy over ordinary permissions or capability changes is not itself identity closure, and the agent&#39;s ability to evolve tools remains S4 rather than ultimate policy. - Evidence: README.md; docs/release-notes/v9.5.19.md; docs/CLI.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: strong security/governance controls do not become S5 without an identity/ultimate-policy issue, legitimate ultimate authority for that issue and a return-to-operation path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f04815ff8e03993c24c7ad49d4093feb09462a74/assessments/bossconsole.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f04815ff8e03993c24c7ad49d4093feb09462a74\">f04815f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-23T00:15:15+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/marcelsud/claude-foreman\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Foreman</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-22\">2026-09-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/marcelsud/claude-foreman/tree/1863451a47f914a909f2b3857a73e87645ccd1fe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1863451a47f914a909f2b3857a73e87645ccd1fe\">1863451 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform bounded implementation work in a target repository through a background Claude Code or Codex worker operating in a Foreman-created worktree. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task-specific repository state, implementation uncertainty, tests/build feedback, local tool outcomes, clarifying questions and review feedback from earlier attempts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive task-local reasoning path, edits and tool actions required to satisfy the assigned implementation prompt within Foreman&#39;s worktree/sandbox/approval envelope. - Decision owner: the autonomous external Claude/Codex worker actor launched through Foreman&#39;s first-party runner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider/model/effort assignment, worker policy, worktree lifecycle, sandboxing, turn budget, exact approval hooks, cancellation, durable events and retry/requeue lifecycle. - Closure path: manager admits a task → detached scheduler claims it → Foreman prepares an isolated worktree and launches the selected external worker → worker makes task-local decisions and receives repository/tool feedback → worker reports completion/failure → Foreman runs verification and returns the result to metasystem review; requeue feedback can enter a later worker attempt. - Boundary reachability: background task creation and execution are the primary shipped product path documented in the repository README and developer reference; runner.py directly launches Claude/Codex worker integrations under the normal daemon workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party control plane owns admission, lifecycle and constraints, but the model actor owns substantive implementation reasoning. Foreman&#39;s deterministic scheduler, sandbox and policy hooks do not choose the code-level solution. - Evidence: README.md; plugins/foreman/README.md; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/runner.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the internal organizational functions of Claude Code/Codex are not inherited. The positive state credits the reachable external operational actor only for the task-local decision right Foreman actually delegates to it.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no installation-level S2 mutual-adjustment function is established from the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Foreman can run multiple tasks, isolate them in worktrees, sequence dependency-gated phases and keep a shared-worktree workflow history linear, but the inspected standard paths do not establish a concrete sibling-generated oscillation/interference signal together with a coordination decision/feedback loop whose function is to damp that disturbance between active S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at the declared recursion. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-task worktrees, dependency records, atomic ready-task claiming, reviewed workflow phase gates, priority and worker-concurrency limits provide isolation/sequencing/resource structure without independently satisfying S2. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no qualifying S2 function was established, so there is no S2-specific decision right to classify. - Evidence: plugins/foreman/README.md; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/SKILL.md; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/references/workflow-schema.md; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/database.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: dependency scheduling can be part of S2 in another boundary if evidence ties it to a concrete inter-S1 disturbance. Here the documentation states dependency/prerequisite and linear-history semantics, not a distinct sensed sibling disturbance and corrective mutual-adjustment loop. Separate worktrees are preventive isolation rather than sufficient S2 evidence by themselves.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate the current Foreman organization by deciding task admission/configuration, routine approvals, responses to actionable worker events, review/requeue/accept outcomes and cancellation/goal state while preserving human authority over critical actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: queued/running/blocked tasks, approval requests, worker errors, failed verification, incomplete diffs, model/provider choices, dependency readiness, cancellations and risky actions can require whole-goal current intervention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: base mode — the autonomous managing Claude/Codex session chooses task definitions/configuration, routine exact approvals, monitoring responses, review feedback, requeue or acceptance; parent mode — the human user decides human-only critical approval requests and can direct/cancel/withhold acceptance through the manager interface. - Decision owner: base mode — the autonomous external managing assistant explicitly assigned the manager role by the shipped Foreman skill and MCP interface. Parent mode — the human user for critical actions and direct supervisory decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: compact task/goal views, actionable taskwait wake events, durable event/approval state, exact hash-bound approval API, scheduler state, task configure/cancel, goal status, task diff, verification summary, requeue/accept transitions and daemon controls. - Closure path: Foreman exposes current goal/task/approval/verification state → managing assistant or human identifies a present-control issue → decision is made through first-party MCP controls → scheduler/runner/database changes current task/approval/goal state → durable events/wake surfaces return the new current state to the manager. - Boundary reachability: the root README instructs users to install the plugin into Claude Code/Codex and start a manager session; the shipped skill explicitly says the managing session is responsible for scope, approvals and final review, and mcpserver.py exposes the corresponding current-control tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the autonomous manager path is function-specific rather than inherited from generic model capability: the shipped skill instructs it to obtain an operational snapshot, wait on actionable events, decide scoped approvals, inspect diffs, requeue defects and accept complete work through Foreman&#39;s MCP tools. Deterministic scheduler/state transitions enforce decisions but do not own them. Human-only categories establish a distinct parent-governed mode. - Evidence: README.md; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/SKILL.md; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/references/approval-policy.md; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/mcpserver.py; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/database.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: queue claiming, dependency release, wake IPC, turn budgets and lifecycle state machines are supporting mechanisms and are not counted as autonomous S3 merely because they supervise execution. The autonomous state is grounded in the shipped manager role plus reachable current-control tools. - Whole-system current view: tasklist/taskget, goal state, actionable event cursors, approval lists, verification summaries, usage and the dashboard expose current task status, worker/model, progress, approvals, dependencies, verification and worktree details across the Foreman-managed goal. - Current-control decision scope: create/configure/cancel tasks; set goal state; decide routine approvals; respond to worker questions; review full diffs/verification; requeue with feedback or accept; start/stop scheduler; escalate human-only risk.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: independently test the worker-produced repository state through declared verification gates and return that complementary evidence into review/rework before acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a worker can report completion while tests/lint/build/read-only Git checks fail, the verification environment errors, or the reviewed snapshot differs from what was actually tested. - Decisive decision or feedback right: define the verification criteria/commands whose independently executed results will challenge the worker&#39;s ordinary completion report before current control accepts or requeues the result. - Decision owner: Foreman does not package an autonomous auditor that chooses the audit criteria. The project/managing assistant supplies verificationcommands (or composes an explicit reviewer phase); Foreman supplies the dedicated independent execution, fingerprinting, persistence and feedback path. Therefore the S3 path is constructor-owned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate verification App Server process, argv-only command execution, workspace-write sandbox, network disabled, bounded output/timeout, worktree fingerprint, durable gate/result records, verifying state, actionable verification-failure events, full diff view and review/requeue/accept transitions. - Closure path: worker finishes and supplies its ordinary result summary → Foreman moves task to verifying → configured gates execute independently against the worker worktree and produce pass/fail/error plus tested-state fingerprint → task moves to awaitingreview with verification summary → manager inspects diff/evidence → failed/incomplete work can be taskrequeued with concrete feedback → Foreman persists review.feedback and injects it into the next worker prompt; accepted work closes the task. - Boundary reachability: verificationcommands are normal task/workflow inputs exposed by the shipped MCP surface and explicitly recommended by the manager skill; runner.py invokes runverificationgates on every finished task that has gates before review. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the verification service is organizationally separate from the worker self-report and its evidence is tied to the tested worktree snapshot, but the meaningful audit judgment is configured by the manager/project rather than selected by an autonomous first-party reviewer. The manager&#39;s later accept/requeue decision is S3 response to audit evidence, not transfer of S3 audit ownership. - Evidence: README.md; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/SKILL.md; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/verification.py; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/runner.py; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/database.py; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/references/workflow-schema.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a reviewed workflow may explicitly add a separate Claude/Codex reviewer phase, but that phase is downstream-composed rather than a mandatory autonomous auditor supplied by Foreman. Its existence reinforces constructor capability rather than changing the standalone state to A. - Claim being audited: the implementation worker&#39;s claim that its finished worktree/result is ready to advance to acceptance. - Ordinary reporting path: external worker completes its task and returns a textual result/summary to runner.py. - Complementary access path: Foreman&#39;s separate verification service executes the configured repository commands directly against the finished worktree and computes a fingerprint of the tested tracked patch plus untracked files. - Independence boundary: verification runs through a distinct Codex App Server command process after worker execution, under its own sandbox/network/output/timeout rules; gate results do not depend on the worker&#39;s self-reported test summary. - Who acts on findings: the managing assistant receives actionable failed-verification/review state, inspects gate output/fingerprint and full diff, then accepts or requeues. Requeue feedback is persisted and returned to the subsequent worker attempt.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no installation-level S4 outside-and-then adaptation loop is established from the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Foreman tracks durable event/usage history, provider/model availability, task outcomes and workflow versions, but the inspected paths do not establish environmental sensing with a prospective future distinction that generates an adaptation option and returns it into present capability/S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S4 at the declared recursion. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable SQLite history, usage summaries, live provider/model readiness checks, workflow versioning, task reconfiguration while queued and manager-authored workflow proposals. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: operational history and current provider availability can inform a manager, but persistence/monitoring/configuration are not a packaged S4 environmental adaptation conversation by themselves. - Evidence: README.md; plugins/foreman/README.md; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/database.py; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/SKILL.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user can ask the external manager to research future options, but generic model capability or ad-hoc prompting is not a standard first-party S4 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no installation-level identity/ultimate-policy closure is established from the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Foreman contains strong safety policy, subscription-only authentication, exact approvals, human-only risk classes, immutable workflow review and explicit no-merge/no-deploy defaults, but these regulate operational authority and risk rather than organizational identity/ultimate policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an identity/ultimate-policy issue. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: approval policy, humanconfirmed critical-action gate, worker policy, sandbox defaults, workflow activation review, model/provider selection and goal status. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: human authority over destructive/external actions is substantial parent governance, but generic final say over operational risk does not satisfy S5 without an identity/ultimate-policy issue and return-to-operation closure at that level. - Evidence: README.md; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/references/approval-policy.md; plugins/foreman/skills/manage-foreman-agents/references/workflow-schema.md; plugins/foreman/src/foreman/mcpserver.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the negative result does not mean Foreman lacks policy. It keeps safety/approval/workflow policy mapped to the operational functions they actually regulate rather than promoting them to S5 from vocabulary or human authority alone.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6d249c0ac2f32c3b180cdab2e30e55899d54ba9c/assessments/foreman.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6d249c0ac2f32c3b180cdab2e30e55899d54ba9c\">6d249c0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T21:48:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Prism-Shadow/penguin-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PenguinHarness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Prism-Shadow/penguin-harness/tree/77c746954f92a588295de1c2dad362b1b8593f19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 77c746954f92a588295de1c2dad362b1b8593f19\">77c7469 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. A configured target agent autonomously performs task work through the Penguin runtime. Its model/tool loop owns the next operational action inside the configured task boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: —. Target, evaluator and optimizer are stages in an evaluation/optimization pipeline, not multiple operational S1 units whose mutual interference is regulated by an S2 function. Parallel evaluation cells and subagent calls are execution structure, not sufficient evidence of S2.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: —. The optimizer changes durable future Agent State after evaluations. That is not a separate whole-system current regulator with authority over shared organizational resources, commitments, priorities or constraints. The historical A promoted optimization authority into S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: A. The evaluator is deliberately separated from the target&#39;s normal production path: the target receives the public Statement but cannot access the private Rubric/Gold/scoring rules, while the evaluator later inspects the isolated workspace, bound root/child traces and the private Rubric to make its own score judgment. That score enters the optimizer&#39;s accept-or-rollback decision, changing subsequent retained Agent State. This establishes a first-party complementary audit channel with autonomous evaluator ownership.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. The optimizer searches improvements against a frozen benchmark using scores and traces from the target&#39;s own task executions. Durable self-improvement is real, but the reviewed path does not establish the required outside-and-then environmental intelligence relation; optimization/learning alone is not S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. The target purpose, benchmark, desired score and optimization initiation are parent-authored constraints. They do not form a runtime identity/ultimate-policy tension-resolution loop with legitimate S5 authority. Parent configuration therefore does not imply P.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/911ebc94216226df3120d8a5762944319ae24529/assessments/penguin-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 911ebc94216226df3120d8a5762944319ae24529\">911ebc9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-06T17:46:29+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/KlaatAI/klaatcode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Klaat Code</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-06\">2026-10-06</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/KlaatAI/klaatcode/tree/b988be7cf6ffe7878e87ed3e720759e0f7247c33\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b988be7cf6ffe7878e87ed3e720759e0f7247c33\">b988be7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously inspect, modify and verify a software project through model-selected coding/tool actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar code, implementation choices, tool/process/test failures, context pressure, permissions, provider/model variation and bounded delegated subtasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose substantive coding/tool actions, decide whether to delegate, interpret returned evidence and determine when the task is complete. - Decision owner: the active model-backed Klaat Code agent; write-capable build/general subagents own their bounded delegated work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry, permissions/sandbox, diagnostics/verification, Plan/Build mode, context compaction, phase/cost guards, sessions, provider routing and hooks/plugins/MCP. - Closure path: user request → model selects direct action or delegated work → first-party runtime executes → results return to the model → model revises/validates or completes. - Boundary reachability: ordinary REPL/headless/server paths instantiate the first-party model/tool loop; \\delegatetask\\ is a built-in model-callable tool. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime code constrains and transports execution, but the model owns open-ended engineering choices and interpretation. - Evidence: README.md; src/screens/repl.ts; src/tools/index.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: hosted Klaatu may perform additional routing, but no hidden hosted function is imported into this public-client assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party inter-S1 coordination loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent write-capable delegated agents can potentially overlap in one project workspace, but no implemented public-client mechanism is shown detecting/attenuating that peer interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persona tool scoping, permission prompts, atomic multi-file patch application and read-only explore/review personas constrain individual operations but do not coordinate peer write-capable S1s. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S2 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main agent may choose multiple delegated tasks, but delegation/concurrency is not itself coordination; roadmap worktree isolation cannot be credited as current runtime behavior. - Evidence: src/agent/personas.ts; src/screens/repl.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: read-only personas are intentionally parallel-safe; that prevents one class of interference by capability restriction but does not establish a general S2 loop for the write-capable operational units.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material whole-system current-control function is established over the live set of delegated operational commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: background child status is observable, but no standard actor has a substantive live control right over individual current child commitments beyond initial delegation and later result use. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S3. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: \\taskstatus\\, live output tails, completion notices, loop limits, phase/cost stops and whole-turn cancellation expose/enforce state without supplying selective live child reallocation. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent agent can spawn and observe children but cannot kill, steer, retry or reprioritize a specific running background child through the reviewed tool surface. - Evidence: src/screens/repl.ts; src/tools/index.ts. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: task visibility is necessary but insufficient; generic cost/loop stops and turn cancellation are enforcement/safeguards rather than whole-system current-control judgment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently inspect a coding claim/change through a separate fresh read-only review agent and return findings to the caller. - Disturbance / variety regulated: bugs, edge cases, security issues, needless complexity and defects missed by the authoring agent&#39;s ordinary coding path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect repository evidence independently and produce severity-ranked findings or a clean judgment. - Decision owner: the dedicated model-backed \\review\\ persona instantiated as a fresh delegated agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated subagent context, read-only tool allowlist, dedicated review system prompt, separate routing tier/loop cap and final-report return to the parent. - Closure path: main agent has a code/change claim → invokes \\delegatetask\\ with \\agent: &quot;review&quot;\\ and a self-contained target → fresh reviewer reads/searches repository evidence → reviewer returns findings → parent agent receives the final report and decides corrective/acceptance action. - Boundary reachability: \\review\\ is an explicit built-in persona and an enumerated value of the model-callable \\delegatetask\\ tool. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit judgment is produced by a separate model context with direct repository-reading tools, not by deterministic linting or the author merely reviewing its own transcript. - Claim being audited: a specified implementation/change or coding claim supplied by the main agent to the review subagent. - Ordinary reporting path: the main/build agent&#39;s own tool results and completion statement. - Complementary access path: the review persona independently uses read/search/code-graph/web-read tools in a fresh context. - Independence boundary: isolated context, dedicated reviewer prompt, read-only capability boundary, no nested delegation and separate model loop. - Who acts on findings: the parent coding agent receives the reviewer final report and owns subsequent repair or acceptance. - Evidence: src/agent/personas.ts; src/tools/index.ts; src/screens/repl.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the slash \\/review\\ path is not needed for this claim; S3 rests on the dedicated fresh delegated reviewer with direct evidence access.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying changing external/future condition is put under an owner that develops adaptation options and returns them into current organizational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S4. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: automatic/session memory distillation, session resume, code graph, web tools, model switching, skills/plugins/MCP and update checks retain or extend capability but do not by themselves close S4. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory is distilled and injected into later sessions, but persistence/reuse of learned facts is not evidence of an external-and-prospective option-development loop returning into current S3 capability. - Evidence: src/agent/memory.ts; src/screens/repl.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: hosted Klaatu routing cannot donate an unobserved S4 function to the public client.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no identity-level policy issue is routed to an authoritative owner and returned as governing policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission rules, sandbox restrictions, project rules, system/persona prompts, config, hooks and Plan approval constrain execution without constituting identity governance. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: coding/review agents operate within user/developer-authored rules and have no authority to redefine Klaat Code&#39;s ultimate identity or governing principles. - Evidence: src/permissions/index.ts; src/agent/personas.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: user approval in Plan/permission flows is operational authority, not an identity/ultimate-policy decision.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/aeb6ed9e6d0d5caddf0fa3797327d1d0613d81af/assessments/klaat-code.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit aeb6ed9e6d0d5caddf0fa3797327d1d0613d81af\">aeb6ed9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T16:00:11+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/xai-org/grok-build\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Grok Build</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/xai-org/grok-build/tree/4247f661689354b831191f11eeeac8424993fe3d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4247f661689354b831191f11eeeac8424993fe3d\">4247f66 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-guided terminal coding actions against a real file/shell/VCS workspace, with live tool-result feedback and continued model turns. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User requirements, changing code/build/test state, tool errors and provider response variability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose and execute task-specific files, shell and code actions and adapt to tool results. - Decision owner: Hosted Grok Build root coding agent with model-guided tool call decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Rust xai-grok-shell agent runtime, model sampler, xai-grok-tools, VCS/workspace interfaces, permission and stop policies. - Closure path: User coding goal → model selects tool/action → tool effect → new observations → model continuation/finish. - Boundary reachability: Official terminal/headless/ACP grok modes activate the model/task/tool Rust agent from the packaged root shell and connect to first-party workspace tool operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The first-party root uses actual task-conditioned model/tool execution; deterministic policy and tool runner restrict, but do not replace, the choice of coding actions. - Evidence: crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/agent/mvpagent/mod.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/mod.rs; README.md - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: ? - Function: Candidate coordination of concurrently operating subagent S1 units; no proven specific cross-child disturbance arbitration beyond quotas/queue and opt-in checkout separation. - Distinct S1 units: Independent child coding-agent tasks with separate subagent identifiers and optional worktrees. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Possible shared-workspace concurrent edits/collisions; source offers isolation but not a proven content-specific arbitration trigger. - Attenuating coordination relation: Bounded coordinator admission and optional worktree isolation are available, but not demonstrated as detecting a particular inter-S1 disturbance and returning corrective coordination. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Child completion/query and queue release inform the parent; a specific cross-child interference remedy is unproven. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: S2 not credited; the observed controls could be generic concurrency limits, session lifecycle and optional separation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Possible overlapping child changes to a shared workspace, exhaustion of concurrency slots, stale child messages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Subagent spawn admission is bounded, and optional isolation=worktree alters child workspace; no definite interference-dependent S2 owner/feedback path located. - Decision owner: Coordinator actor and parent/root choice; function-specific S2 ownership unresolved. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Single-writer coordinator, Admission concurrency ceiling, pending/active queues, worktree option and separate child lifecycle. - Closure path: Child events pass through queued/active lifecycle, but no demonstrated inter-child content-conflict sensor and returned remedial assignment has been established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Concurrency caps, independent child actors and worktree isolation alone are not evidence of a cross-S1, conflict-specific regulation loop. - Evidence: crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/admission.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/coordinator.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/mod.rs - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Root coding agent controls current child task commitments, interrupts and collects returned subordinate results in its own team recursion. - Whole-system current view: Parent/root has child task list, pending/active/completed state and returned outputs in its own session&#39;s bounded team. - Current-control decision scope: Child task commitments, start/stop, future instructions and result integration within one coding-team run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Running, queued or stalled subagent tasks, completion/failure and changes to parent coding goal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model-root may invoke task spawn, inspect running children, await child outputs, send child messages and cancel/redirect child tasks. - Decision owner: Model-driven Grok Build parent/root agent using packaged task and subagent lifecycle tools; coordinator implements these decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task tool, coordinator registry, list/query/kill controls, active message delivery, root/child ownership enforcement. - Closure path: Child progress and completion → coordinator result/query to parent → root model selects further task/message/cancel or integrates child output → changed child activity. - Boundary reachability: First-party Task/subagent query/cancel/message interfaces are wired to the parent agent and coordinator and admit actual independent worker attempts rather than merely showing a static status dashboard. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model root chooses consequential team task commitments based on child outcomes, not merely reading one worker&#39;s progress bar. - Evidence: crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/extensions/task.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/agent/subagent/spawn.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/coordinator/query.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/coordinator.rs - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: No established independently sourced complementary audit of the producing coding agents or team current-operation claims. - Claim being audited: That the child or root-produced code actually fulfills its requested task. - Ordinary reporting path: A completed child returns a result into the parent Task/output and coordinator message path. - Complementary access path: Unresolved; neither cloud inline review comment records nor an optional reviewer persona prove separately observed source/evidence. - Independence boundary: A separately executing auditor with direct artifact sampling and corrective feedback is not established at the declared mode. - Who acts on findings: Parent may act on child output/messages, but no guaranteed complementary audit finding route was identified. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Erroneous developer work, unverified result claims and hidden defects. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separate reviewer persona/agent can be requested as a child, but guaranteed independent direct-source observational audit and corrective return are not established as a shipped built-in closure. - Decision owner: Unresolved; review-comment extension stores UI events and is not an independent auditing agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Subagent task/persona facilities; ACP review extension; standard tool feedback. - Closure path: Review comments/child messages may be consumed by root, but source-to-independent-reviewer audit → corrective control path is unproven. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A review UI event or model-provided reviewer persona is insufficient without materially independent observation and change in current work. - Evidence: crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/extensions/review.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/coordinator.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/backendtests.rs - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Memory, task planning and persistent/long-running sessions are present, but no complete outside-and-then foresight and adaptation closure is established. - External distinction: Workspace and model inputs may refer to external trends or code, without an identified sensing-and-foresight role. - Future / prospective distinction: Persisted memory and long-running tasks affect later operations, but future time alone is not a prospective decision. - Adaptation option generated: Not established as a future organizational capability redesign choice. - Path back into current capability / S3: Memory/session resume can influence subsequent coding decisions; strategic capability-change closure remains unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changed future user coding needs, project architecture and external developments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model can adapt a present coding plan and maintain memory, but capacity-changing future-oriented decision ownership is unresolved. - Decision owner: Unresolved between user/configuration and model decisions on immediate tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: xai-grok-memory, session resume, task tools and configuration. - Closure path: Saved context returns to future model tasks, without an established external prospective option/apply loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Persistent memory or near-term task planning is not automatically VSM S4. - Evidence: crates/codegen/xai-grok-memory/src/lib.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/agent/mvpagent/mod.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/extensions/task.rs - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: ? - Function: Product identity, permissions and policy constrain operations but do not yet establish a function-specific ultimate-policy closure. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: What Grok Build root/team is ultimately allowed to do and for whom. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: No confirmed ultimate-policy actor at this recursion; session credentials and user permission gates exist but do not qualify by themselves. - Return-to-operation path: Sandbox and permission decisions filter tool actions, without a demonstrated complete governing policy feedback path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User trust, sandbox boundaries, resource access and model policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Runtime and human user authorize particular actions; no definitive highest-policy actor and feedback reorganization demonstrated. - Decision owner: Unresolved at project coding-team recursion; user permission enforcement is not enough to credit S5=P. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Sandbox restrictions, workspace permissions, client authentication and configuration. - Closure path: Allowed/denied actions change execution, but an ultimate identity/policy choice returned across organizational operation is not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Enforcing sandbox or workspace permissions is narrower than ultimate organizational policy authority. - Evidence: crates/codegen/xai-grok-sandbox/src/lib.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/agent/mvpagent/mod.rs; crates/codegen/xai-grok-tools/src/implementations/grokbuild/task/rootcontrol.rs - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e5406871ace31d3eb4a8b9394d2b0f5deebbafc0/assessments/grok-build.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e5406871ace31d3eb4a8b9394d2b0f5deebbafc0\">e540687 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-17T19:26:33+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kstawiski/evidence-gated-scientific-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Evidence Bench</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-17\">2026-10-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kstawiski/evidence-gated-scientific-agent/tree/85b396a824dfad6ec0880cbe07d86d3e24920ae8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 85b396a824dfad6ec0880cbe07d86d3e24920ae8\">85b396a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform research, produce a plan/report and generate grounded scientific evidence through actual model-directed source/tool interaction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain scientific question, varying source data and code results, inconsistent literature, missing provenance and model hallucinations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Qwen/Gemma planning models develop concrete alternative scientific plans, synthesizer selects a MasterPlan; first-party model researcher and reporter take bounded retrieval/computation/tool steps to produce a scientific report. Repair models use new observed findings to generate next revised outputs. - Decision owner: First-party ADK/model role logic invoked by runscientifictask; external inference is a configured backend, not an outside authored research operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan proposal/research/report prompts, ADK workflow, scientific ToolPolicy, retrieval evidence ledger, typed computation tools and deterministic lints. - Closure path: objective/inputs → independent model plan candidates and auditable master → model/tool research and computation observations → model report with cited outputs → critique feedback → model repair/new report. - Boundary reachability: The shipped orchestrator&#39;s runscientifictask calls buildplanningworkflow / runtyped, then producereport, which creates configured first-party ADK research/report toolsets. The same entry is reached through exported run and services. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model planners/researchers select substantive plan/evidence and responsive report/repair content from observed sources and tool results; static control and policy guardrails are separate. - Evidence: scientificagent/workflow.py; scientificagent/orchestrator.py; scientificagent/runtime.py; scientificagent/execution.py. - Basis: Reachable source-level model/tool/evidence-feedback implementation. - Confidence: High on installed loops, not measured scientific correctness or model inference. - Caveats: Actual Qwen/Gemma backend and any enabled optional code or MCP tools must be configured; benchmarking claims not imported.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No demonstrated control attenuating a material inter-S1 operational collision rather than merely collecting parallel proposals and sequencing task stages. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two planners may provide different suggestions, but they have no shared mutable working target or independently competing active operational commitment requiring stabilization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Qwen synthesizes proposals into one plan; deterministic serial stage/gates select what comes next. Neither path detects and resolves interference among independent ongoing S1 operations. - Decision owner: Planning join/synthesizer and single-run controller. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ADK maxconcurrency=2 parallel planner generation, JoinNode, typed candidate collection, and final deterministic lint. - Closure path: Two plan alternatives → synthesis → master plan, which is task content formation. There is no distinct operating-unit clash → anti-interference choice → changed sibling activity feedback documented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A comparison of concurrently generated plans, their disagreement and synthesis is not by itself a Beer S2 loop, nor do research budgets alone prove inter-S1 mutual correction. - Evidence: scientificagent/workflow.py; scientificagent/orchestrator.py; scientificagent/policy.py. - Basis: Bounded negative assessment of candidate planning/phase-control mechanisms. - Confidence: Medium: deliberate independent planning and synthesis is source-real, but no specific inter-S1 disturbance/attenuation contract was found. - Caveats: A deployment with multiple simultaneous runs may add coordination in a separate server layer; not credited without source proof for the assessed run.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: Whole-current scientific run control: when plans are accepted, which research/evidence/critique phase may proceed, how scarce model/tool/repair attempts are budgeted and when final outputs are supported or withheld. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unreliable plans, unsupported evidence, unbounded tool/model calls, failing audit findings, code/tool nonprogress and bounded available repair resources. - Decisive decision or feedback right: runscientifictask orchestrates planning/admissibility, reassigns rejected plans to repair, conditionally admits research, enforces research budgets, orders deterministic validation and independent reviewer, initiates limited repair, and sets final status rather than promoting rejected reports. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic Python controller and configured ResearchBudgetController, not autonomous scientific principal or another model owning organization-wide allocation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Planning.status, MaxRepairRounds, ToolPolicy, sandbox restrictions, deterministic lints, evidence/provenance ledger, needsrepair and finalrunstatus. - Closure path: Current plan/evidence/critic state and resource use → controller selects stop, execute next unit or bounded repair → new operations/results → updated audit+status → repeat or present final result/needs-human with recorded reasons. - Whole-system current view: Active master plan, model research state, evidence/retrieval and computation, deterministic validity, separate audit report, cumulative tool counts and attempts across one full scientific run. - Current-control decision scope: The constructor controls operational commitments of specialized planning, research/reporting, critique and revision operations, their admissibility and their shared run budget. - Boundary reachability: runscientifictask allocates ResearchBudgetController, executes prepareandauditplan, checks planning.status before producereport, validates outputs, invokes independent report review and loops repairs while needsrepair and the configured round cap hold; finalrunstatus controls final admission. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Decisive phase/budget/stop choices are expressed by static controller rules rather than a free model explicitly managing whole-current resource allocation; S3=C, not S3=A. - Evidence: scientificagent/orchestrator.py; scientificagent/workflow.py; scientificagent/policy.py; scientificagent/config.py. - Basis: Source-attested current-commitment controller with feedback from multiple first-party operating roles. - Confidence: High for constructor current-run regulation; no external scientific-team budget governance claimed. - Caveats: Single-run scope is not project portfolio governance, and some phases use the same model provider for different prompts.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently challenge active research plan and report claims from an alternative model/evidence path, with critic findings feeding real repair or an unresolved stop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsound scientific plans, citations without source support, fabricated computation values, contradictory data/display, incorrect visual readings and unsupported final conclusions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separately instructed Gemma PLANAUDITOR audits the Qwen-generated MasterPlan; independent REPORTAUDITOR evaluates claimed results against retrieved/computed artifact evidence and deterministic validation, while visual review can inspect actual figure/table pixel inputs. Critic verdict/blocking findings can force a repair or prevent supported terminal status. - Decision owner: First-party distinct model-based Gemma critic invocation and structured VerificationReport judgment; constructor separately validates/reconciles the critic&#39;s outputs and commits control transitions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan audit packet with independently generated checklist, retrieval/computation evidence, detached visual bytes, deterministic validators, unique audit artifacts and bounded repair envelopes. - Closure path: Qwen plan/report claim → independent Gemma plan/report/artifact review → structured blocking/nonblocking findings → controller&#39;s planningstatus or needsrepair chooses repair/withholding → revised plan/report and re-audit → supported/needs-human termination. - Claim being audited: Whether the plan&#39;s scientific method is admissible and whether report conclusions/displays are justified by gathered evidence. - Ordinary reporting path: Qwen research/reporter structured scientific report, planned steps and source/output claims. - Complementary access path: Gemma uses separately instructed audit prompts and controller-provided source/compute/citation/validation evidence plus actual rasterized displays, rather than merely accepting report narrative or self-score. - Independence boundary: Distinct model-critic role and alternative evidence/report read path; not a different OS trust domain, protected human team or globally independent provider. Strictly bounded to enabled first-party run configuration. - Who acts on findings: Constructor prepareandauditplan and report repair loop consume Gemma&#39;s verdict and findings; repairplan/ producereport receive them, final finalrunstatus refuses supported unless independent review is passing. - Boundary reachability: buildplanningworkflow invokes auditmasterplan on the actual master, and runscientifictask invokes auditreportresilient on the actual report/retrieval/computation. planningstatus, needsrepair and final status consume those outcomes to change installed execution, not merely save benchmark scores. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The alternative critical reading and structured falsification findings are selected by a model with separate audit instructions and evidence packet; deterministic controller owns repair scheduling/format admission but not the semantic critic judgment. S3=A, not just C. - Evidence: scientificagent/workflow.py; scientificagent/orchestrator.py; scientificagent/prompts.py; scientificagent/linting.py. - Basis: Source-reachable independent critic alternate evidence with actual correction-return and bounded blocking. - Confidence: High for model-based audit and repair wiring, medium for effectiveness of critic independence and coverage. - Caveats: On model outage or exhaustion the controller can return inconclusive/needs-human rather than supported. Model critic has its own error modes and deterministic checks sometimes reject contradictory critic instructions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No established prospective external strategic adaptation of the scientific organization&#39;s own future capability returned into whole-current S3. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Scientific literature discoveries, new input datasets and computational results are changing task environment, but are handled as evidence for the present user research question. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The agents retrieve literature and propose revised scientific methods, yet no first-party function evaluates externally changing opportunities or future organizational capabilities and installs a choice back into ongoing scientific-agent governance. - Decision owner: Model research/plan production and constructor task validation, not a separate future-capability adaptation function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Literature acquisition, knowledge library, revision tasks, plan synthesis/repair, model and tool configuration. - Closure path: External paper/data → current study evidence/plan → accepted report or within-task repair. No organizational environment foresight → comparative capability options → enduring adaptation choice → current S3 return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Scientific research may explore future hypotheses, but that is S1 work for a requested scientific study, not automatic evidence of this harness changing its own future operational repertoire. - Evidence: scientificagent/literature.py; scientificagent/workflow.py; scientificagent/orchestrator.py; scientificagent/knowledge.py. - Basis: Bounded negative review of strongest apparent prospective literature and revision flows. - Confidence: Medium-high within one-run recursion. - Caveats: External researchers/operators can improve prompts/tools in later repository revisions; those human activities are outside the reviewed deployed agent organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No owned ultimate policy/identity/ethos settlement path distinguished from fixed scientific integrity and tool safety rules. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Scientific risk labels, provenance obligations, tool permissions, domain/language constraints, evaluation failure and human reconsideration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Task requester and first-party configured controller impose a risk-sensitive scientific protocol and locked task constraints; on exhausted attempts the controller reports requireshumandecision, but it does not adjudicate ultimate organization identity/policy conflicts. - Decision owner: Human scientific owner outside runtime for ultimate risk and approval; fixed first-party admission rules for current-run integrity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MasterPlan/task protocol binding, ToolPolicy, static scientific safety rules, sandbox defaults, provenance manifest and risk classifications. - Closure path: Inputs/risk constraints → fixed scientific gate and possible human escalation. No first-party policy dispute → ultimate legitimate purpose/ethos decision → binding return to organizational governance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Fail-closed status, provenance hashes, immutable task locks and user escalation are robust current-run controls, not a system deciding its ultimate goals. - Evidence: scientificagent/workflow.py; scientificagent/orchestrator.py; scientificagent/policy.py; docs/MVPSCOPE.md. - Basis: Bounded negative review of policy/risk/identity surfaces. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A human scientific review board may have ultimate policy authority outside this product; no inherited S5 parent relation is scored without a first-party exposed closing loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/066ea03ff0b6e663b615313395c5ba10bf1c9f8a/assessments/evidence-bench.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 066ea03ff0b6e663b615313395c5ba10bf1c9f8a\">066ea03 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-13T22:09:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/whycorporation/whycodes\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WhyCodes</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-13\">2026-10-13</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/whycorporation/whycodes/tree/ef5126824739673d93815b29456ba3e5bdddf87c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ef5126824739673d93815b29456ba3e5bdddf87c\">ef51268 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: take discretionary local codebase engineering actions and repairs via first-party model-selected tools, and independently execute model-owned delegated subtask work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code state, compilation/test failures, incomplete edits, missing information and live tool error observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the root or child coding model chooses its own repository read/edit/shell operations and responds to real outcomes. - Decision owner: active language-model agent within the standard runtime; a spawned general worker has its own model/tool context. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: turn loop, tool/permission gate, local session and persistent history, code tools and optional subagent process/worktree. - Closure path: engineering request → model-selected tools → actual local execution/result → next tool/repair choice or completion. - Boundary reachability: normal whycodes agent loop and first-party registered task/swarm, not only tests. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: fixed dispatch/sandbox and memory cannot choose a context-sensitive code change without the model&#39;s discretionary action. - Evidence: README.md; turn.rs; gate.rs; dispatch.rs; subagent.rs; mod.rs - Basis: explicit / structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Distinct S1 units: concurrent first-party model-running general or other delegated coding workers in one swarm task wave; each has independent model turn/tool decisions and an assigned scoped goal. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two worker S1s can claim/write an overlapping source file in the same checkout, observe an out-of-date version of another worker&#39;s changed file, or contribute overlapping changes that would overwrite diverged main checkout content when merging isolated worktrees. - Attenuating coordination relation: FileClaimRegistry refuses double ownership, logs/reports stale reads and actively emits FileConflict; preclaims reject inconsistent paths before work; default separate worktrees quarantine writes and three-way guarded merge declines conflicting paths rather than silently clobbering main. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a conflicting same-checkout writer receives a tool denial/conflict owner, an attempted preclaim returns an immediate error to parent, and worktree conflicts appear in worker/aggregate results so the parent can narrow scopes or retry/reassign. Nonconflicting file changes are applied to the main checkout. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: these first-party checks regulate concrete multi-S1 interference over the same file&#39;s ownership or merged effects, not merely launch tasks or display messages. - Function: attenuate source-file/merge interference among multiple independently model-running coding S1 workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent overlapping file writes, stale reads and conflicting post-worktree integration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: deterministic same-checkout file-claim gate and three-way merge decide whether a conflicting write or integration may proceed; conflict event/result names the current owner/path. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party coordination constructor (C), not an autonomous S2 model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shared registry, worker-specific context, read/write tool hooks, preclaim checks, worktree creation/merge, conflict events and status reports. - Closure path: independent workers approach same source path → exclusive claim or guarded merge compares ownership/base/main → unsafe action is blocked or marked conflicted → affected worker or root receives conflict information → subsequent work can adopt safe scoped action/repair. - Boundary reachability: types.rs default enables swarm/worktrees; gate.rs and dispatch.rs execute code-owned swarm entrypoint and claims/merge in supported modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: interference-specific admission/merge outcome is coded and remains if model decision-making is removed; the model may respond as S3 but does not own the fixed S2 conflict rule. - Evidence: swarm.rs; fileclaims.rs; swarmworktree.rs; dispatch.rs; subagent.rs; swarmhub.rs; modtests.rs - Basis: explicit first-party code paths and regression tests - Confidence: high for bounded technical write/merge conflict regulation - Caveats: same-checkout conflicts are observed only through claimed file operations; undeclared semantic conflicts and independent manually launched sessions are not guaranteed covered. Worktree conflicts can remain for manual/follow-on resolution.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: the root&#39;s swarm result aggregates every invoked worker&#39;s type, goal, completion/failure, duration, output, merge conflicts and applied file effects into one current-wave report; the parent retains the ongoing model conversation and tool outcomes. - Current-control decision scope: root model selects an initial set of worker roles, scope/path ownership and concurrency, then can elect an additional/corrective wave, change active task scope assignments for the next wave, or directly repair integration issues based on aggregate current worker status/conflicts. - Function: manage the current set of delegated coding commitments through model-selected work partitioning and evidence-based follow-on assignment/correction between waves. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uneven worker completion, failed tasks, merge conflicts, integration tradeoffs and current coding workload allocation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: root model selects which present work to assign to which subordinate type and scope, and how to revise current remaining work after observing whole-wave results. - Decision owner: top-level model over the supported first-party swarm mode, not fixed semaphore/worktree merge code. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: swarm task spec/tool contract, aggregate report with per-member status/conflicts, model turn tool-result return, and S2 guarded application. - Closure path: model assigns a concurrent worker roster → independent workers act and current report returns including failures/conflicts → model can choose changed worker brief/scope, follow-up task or repair → next first-party tool/run applies the revised commitment. - Boundary reachability: default-enabled first-party swarm in actual Agent::runturn execution, not an unwired utility or example. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic worker scheduling and reporting cannot determine appropriate future current-job assignments or repairs absent the root model&#39;s discretion. - Evidence: swarm.rs; swarm.rs; dispatch.rs; gate.rs; turn.rs; types.rs; subagent.rs - Basis: structural, with supported tool-model response loop - Confidence: medium for wave-boundary model-control closure, no live provider session run - Caveats: manager sees consolidated wave results after workers settle; real-time mid-wave organizational reprioritization/preemption and whole-deployment resource policy are not claimed. This is current multi-worker job management, not prospective S4.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no shipped separately owned complementary reviewer established to independently inspect an implementing S1&#39;s current completion claim and return a corrective verdict to the first-party S3 current manager. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect reported code completion or regressions accepted without a separate direct-evidence challenge. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independent reviewer verdict/return path within assessed standard modes. A swarm report describes worker outcomes; file merge check detects structural conflicts but not independent semantic quality of coding claims. - Decision owner: implementing coding model and the root can run ordinary tests/read code; optional human/CI review remains outside system. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: worktree guarded merge, file-claim conflict reports, generic read-only scout/explore workers, permission and test tools. - Closure path: results are reported to parent coding model; no first-party dedicated independent reviewer claim check → manager judgment → required corrective operation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic worker read-only capability or worktree merge safety does not become a separate source-first complementary auditor by labeling output an audit. - Evidence: dispatch.rs; swarm.rs; subagent.rs; swarmworktree.rs; README.md; mod.rs - Basis: structural review of shipped roles/wiring -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no future-oriented environmental intelligence and applied organizational capability adaptation owner demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future coding environments, model/provider capability shifts and new classes of tooling. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no model or legitimate parent selects organizational future capabilities then returns an implemented change. - Decision owner: user/config/developers set provider/workflow/skills; root handles present coding work and current worker allocation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: memory, session compaction, skill registry, provider routing, SDK/daemon interface, schedule and contextual tools. - Closure path: configuration and memory inform current actions, but future environmental sensing → strategic capability selection → applied new operating capacity is not wired as one ownership cycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: adding workers to the current coding task or recalling previous files is S1/S3 present control, not S4 organizational adaptation. - Evidence: README.md; architecture.md; lib.rs; mod.rs; types.rs - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party ultimate organizational mission/identity policy authority with binding corrective return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: choice over organizational purpose/constitutional priorities, beyond acceptance of individual code edits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no legitimate S5 owner sets/reconciles final mission values and returns binding governing change to subordinate organizational functions. - Decision owner: operator/project owners configure prompts, tool capabilities and permissions; model selects execution within the declared scope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission policies and user approvals, tool/shell sandbox, session roles, inherited worker restrictions. - Closure path: risky tool actions allowed, denied or paused under user/developer rules; neither constitutes an organizational identity-policy choice nor changes the system&#39;s governing purpose. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: per-tool allowed/denied and sandbox boundaries enforce action safety, not ultimate organization policy. - Evidence: types.rs; mod.rs; gate.rs; subagent.rs; README.md - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/20907c5be09105accc3e1e28053abcab39b2f98d/assessments/whycodes.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 20907c5be09105accc3e1e28053abcab39b2f98d\">20907c5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-20T04:21:03+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Hyper-AI-Lab/cyber-ai-team\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Cyber AI Team</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-20\">2026-10-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Hyper-AI-Lab/cyber-ai-team/tree/525401e40f34863cc7f4d3b053d437ce7709912f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 525401e40f34863cc7f4d3b053d437ce7709912f\">525401e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Continuing, bounded AI business-domain operations: domain-role agents process routed company events and enduring mandate backlog work, reason from current company evidence, propose actions and follow-up tasks, and return durable outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incoming CRM/finance/support/operations events, conflicting or stale role/memory claims, evidence gaps, unavailable tools/providers, workflow failures and limits on action authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Registered, configured model agents in AgentManager.invokeagent choose structured assessments/recommendedaction/proposedwork or one of trusted action-candidate references using the first-party LLMGateway. The domain loop leases assigned work, invokes model analysis, grounds its claims and may create evidence-bound follow-up work or request an owner escalation. Direct external side effects are mediated by separate action policy/approval. - Decision owner: First-party AI role bound by durable AgentMandate and DomainAutonomyControl; the LLM owns semantic bounded work choices, while deterministic WorkPortfolio validates scope, evidence and persistence and the parent owner controls high-impact authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: RoleManifest/Agent records, model gateway, memory protocol and authoritative ERPNext/context snapshots, per-domain mandate and work leases, WorkPortfolioService.executeadvisorywork, grounded output schema and follow-up creation, outcome service. - Closure path: ERPNext/other company signal or continuing domain mandate → BusinessEvent router assigns a BusinessWorkItem → domain-role LLM assesses current trusted company context and recommends work/action → grounding/action-policy gate → task result, new work, evidence, owner attention or blocked decision → returned domain-loop state/next cycles. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Packaged default role families, AgentManager LLM invoke and WorkPortfolioService.runalldomainloops/executeadvisorywork are wired into AutonomousCompanyCycleService.run. A configured supported real LLM provider is required; the decisive role task choice is not merely a static role inventory or offline test fixture. - Evidence: Without a model role&#39;s current evidence-based reasoning the system still could route events and manage leases, but could not autonomously decide nuanced domain next work and trusted candidate selection; this is source-backed S1 inside limited authority. - Basis: backend/src/cyberteam/agents/manager.py; backend/src/cyberteam/llm/gateway.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/workportfolio.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/autonomycycle.py; backend/src/cyberteam/roles/defaults.yaml; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/actionpolicy.py; backend/src/cyberteam/memory/protocol.py. - Confidence: First-party business-domain LLM work execution code rather than claims from diagrams or hypothetical integrations. - Caveats: High for agent-owned advisory/internal domain S1; no live ERPNext/provider deployment benchmark. - undefined: Most first-party model-produced outputs are advisory and some safe actions can be applied only after detailed policy checks. This assessment does not assume blanket autonomous external purchases, messages, trades or legal authority. - Boundary reachability: backend/src/cyberteam/agents/manager.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/workportfolio.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/autonomycycle.py: the first-party production run chain registers actual model-driven agents, invokes them on continuing role queues and stores/feeds their results back to business-domain operation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No separate evidence-backed agent-owned mutual disturbance-damping function between ongoing viable S1 domains, despite real first-party global event routing, duplicate suppression and per-domain backlog caps. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Company events may affect finance, support, engineering and sales in different ways; duplicate work, growing task queues and resource contention risk oscillations or work collisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: First-party WorkPortfolio routes classified events to domains and deterministically suppresses duplicate work, excessive proposal depth and per-domain backlog overflow. This is shared task admission/load shedding under fixed rules; it does not delegate independent S2 judgment to negotiate conflicts or return reciprocal compromises between distinct operating domain units. - Decision owner: Deterministic router, queue lease/capacity/stabilizer and host configuration, not a separately authorized inter-S1 coordinating decision owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DomainRegistry mappings, routependingevents, stabilizedomainbacklogs, BusinessEventDelivery, role mandates, domain capacity controls and work idempotency. - Closure path: Event→one routed role work item; over-limit or semantically duplicate proposal→deterministic cancellation; task result→work status. No distinct domain-vs-domain interference report→S2 negotiation/decision→modified reciprocal commitments to affected domain roles. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Continual domain roles and mandates are more than a one-shot DAG, so S2 was explicitly tested. But control acts as deterministic per-event/per-domain bookkeeping, not an agent owned cross-domain disturbance regulator with evidentially returned decisions to multiple units. - Evidence: backend/src/cyberteam/operations/workportfolio.py; backend/src/cyberteam/company/domainregistry.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/operatingmodel.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/governor.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/autonomycycle.py. - Basis: Bounded negative after inspecting highest-potential cross-domain shared queue and stabilizer. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The product may help owners coordinate domains manually or compose later arbitration. Neither is credited as the independent autonomous S2 ownership mode in this pinned source.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Inside-and-now company current control of independently continuing S1 business domains: owner chooses which business domain may operate, be paused or taken over and returns the decision to domain-worker execution; parent-governed operational mode is closed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe autonomous operations in a domain, misconfigured capability/role, changing trust in company evidence, required human takeover, conflicts of authority, failed domain activity or unacceptable risk. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Authenticated owner makes binding per-domain active | paused | takeover decision with a reason, recorded as DomainControlRevision and DomainAutonomyControl; subsequent rundomainloop explicitly prevents work when state is not active, and runalldomainloops admits only active domain roles. The owner has a present operational intervention right across continuing domain S1s, not merely a generic approve-this-tool button. - Decision owner: Legitimate authenticated human company owner through a first-party PUT /domain-controls/{domain} route requiring resource-specific authorization, WorkPortfolioService.updatedomaincontrol and persistent owner revisions. Automated operating model reconciler / governor enforce policy and state but do not themselves own a separate evidenced discretionary autonomous S3; publication P. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Principal/getcurrentprincipal and requireauthorization, DomainControlRevision and DomainAutonomyControl, owner lock, WorkPortfolioService and per-domain run loop, resumption signals and audit control evidence; deterministic government snapshots and role status. - Closure path: Owner observes operating domain/roles/readiness from console → sends authenticated active/paused/takeover decision with cause → service records owner-locked current control revision and effective domain state → later domain task leasing stops or resumes, and domain state is auditable/visible → owner can revise decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Parent mode can run within the shipped self-hosted company OS: the API route directly calls updatedomaincontrol, commits owner-marked revision, and the first-party continuing domain-role loop checks effective control before leasing work. No unpublished owner-agent code is needed. - Evidence: A fixed router/threshold is not by itself S3; the distinctive decisive choice here is the owner&#39;s discretionary current intervention over multiple persistent autonomous operating domains, with a first-party returned effect on their operation. - Basis: backend/src/cyberteam/api/routes/operations.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/workportfolio.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/operatingmodel.py; backend/src/cyberteam/db/models.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/executive.py; backend/src/cyberteam/authorization/service.py. - Confidence: Specific first-party parent decision/return at existing multi-domain operational recursion, separate from governance merely via approval buttons. - Caveats: High for closed S3=P intervention mode; not an S3=A claim. - undefined: Authorization ensures an authenticated principal with permission; actual company ownership and legitimate policy are deployment supplied. The control is domain active/pause/takeover and is not proof of unrestricted owner allocation of financial budgets or every company-wide executive decision. - Boundary reachability: backend/src/cyberteam/api/routes/operations.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/workportfolio.py: authenticated owner domain-control decision persisted in actual revision/effective control and consumed by continuing role work loops; supported mode is packaged API/service, not only a suggested integration. - Whole-system current view: The owner console and listdomaincontrols expose the whole registered operating-domain set, effective states and revision IDs; the Governor snapshot/operating model adds mandates, workflows, gaps and current readiness. - Current-control decision scope: Binding owner decision to pause, take over or release continuing business-domain S1 work; this changes immediate operating authorization rather than merely reporting state or limiting a single task&#39;s tool. - Parent-mode decisive owner: Authenticated company owner using a resource-scoped authorized HTTP operation, not the deterministic decide Governor rules or a model recommendation. - Parent-mode trigger and closure: Owner observes unacceptable/current domain condition → chooses active/paused/takeover with reason → saved DomainControlRevision (owner-locked) → per-domain loop blocks/allows future work, confirmed in list/readiness and audit → owner can revise the state. - Mode distinction: Parent-governed P only; no separate autonomous S3 A or autonomous-actor-composition S3 C is asserted at this boundary.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No autonomous complementary S3 audit with independent direct operational reality access challenging the owner/current-management account and returning a corrective management conclusion, despite substantive Observer/data consistency checks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Governor may make poor decisions from incomplete snapshots, company strategy proposals may rely on stale claims, role models may assert incorrect current staff/ERPNext state, and action approvals may be unsafe. - Decisive decision or feedback right: ObserverReview checks proposed strategy/operating model against saved evidence, schemas, authority and model confidence and can block changes; grounding verifier can quarantine contradictions with trusted role records. These inspect governed proposals and internal reports, not independently commission a complementary test of S3 parent&#39;s entire current-state claims against a separate field reality route, then deliver enforceable correction to the owner. - Decision owner: Deterministic Observer validators and first-party authoritative-state/claims gates. ObserverAgent is named, but this code does not establish an independently authorized S3 auditor with distinct field observation and return to parent S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OperatingModelLifecycleService.reviewrevision and reviewfindings, CompanyStrategyService.recordobserverreview, executive ObserverReview, WorkPortfolio claim grounding, memory conflict quarantine, read-only audit/event storage. - Closure path: Proposed operating model/strategy → Observer validates same persisted evidence and marks agreed or ownerreview → reconciliation blocked/allowed. S3 owner domain control remains separately managed; no independent S3 report test at actual domain operations→audit finding→binding owner S3 corrective response. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A second deterministic look at the same data can guard unsafe plans and remain operationally useful; audit independence and complementary access to current organizational field truth are not proven by a separate &#39;Observer&#39; class or approval gate. - Evidence: backend/src/cyberteam/operations/operatingmodel.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/strategy.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/executive.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/workportfolio.py; backend/src/cyberteam/audit/service.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/supervisorreview.py. - Basis: Bounded absence after reviewing all plausible independent validation and critical-observer modes. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: An independent real-world evaluator could use the saved evidence or ERPNext data, but is not shown as shipped S3 mandate and owner-return.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Outside-and-then prospective company intelligence and adaptation: acquire/research changing external company evidence, generate measurable strategic objectives/KPIs/experiments and feed accepted strategy into the next actual business operating model and mandates. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown market/customer/offerings facts, changes in external company evidence, emerging company roles/capability gaps, KPI performance drift and changing future priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: First-party CompanyIntelligenceService acquires evidence, builds an active company model and resolves unknowns; CompanyStrategyService invokes LLM to propose future objectives, KPI formulas and reversible experiments from provenance claims, validates proposals, subjects them to Observer gate, then commits active/probationary revisions and retires stale alternatives. Subsequent OperatingModelLifecycleService synthesizes active objectives and KPI revisions and reconciles business domains/role mandates. - Decision owner: Model-backed Strategy Portfolio Agent owns proposed future option content; validating Observer and deterministic policy own admission constraints; authorized human parent remains owner of high-impact external actions. The first-party agent-driven future option selection/feedback loop is closed for low-risk proposed strategy without an additional user request. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Evidence acquisition/discovery/research, CompanyModelRevision, LLM strategy proposals and validation, observer activation verdict, objective/KPI/experiment revision writes, current state reuse, operating model source hash and reconcile/convergerolesandmandates, periodic autonomous company cycle. - Closure path: Company/ERPNext/public external evidence → active company model/claims → model produces forward-looking discovery/strategic goals and reversible experiments → evidence+Observer validation accepts or escalates → active strategy revisions stored → next operatingmodel.synthesize consumes revisions and reconciles actual domains and role mandates → domain loops receive changed work/capabilities. Same source event no-ops on unchanged strategy context hash. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The published AutonomousCompanyCycleService.run calls acquisition, discovery, strategy.runstrategycycle, operatingmodel.synthesizeandreview/reconcile/convergerolesandmandates, and runalldomainloops in that order; this is a tangible end-to-end return path from model-selected future options into continuing operations. - Evidence: Unlike static cron triggers or one-case retrospective memory, the LLM chooses new strategic objectives/experiments on separately acquired company evidence and the code installs accepted objective revisions into the company operating-model source of truth. - Basis: backend/src/cyberteam/operations/autonomycycle.py; backend/src/cyberteam/company/intelligence.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/strategy.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/operatingmodel.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/workportfolio.py; backend/src/cyberteam/db/models.py. - Confidence: First-party model-assisted future option selection, evidence gate, durable adoption and operational return; does not assert quality of predictions. - Caveats: High for source-reachable S4=A (conditional on a configured provider and active verified company model). - undefined: Active model and Observer agreement are hard prerequisites; failed inference or uncertain provenance blocks option admission. Not evidence of live external market intelligence success or high financial returns. Owner-approved high impact external changes are separate and no distinct S4(P) mode asserted. - Boundary reachability: backend/src/cyberteam/operations/autonomycycle.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/strategy.py; backend/src/cyberteam/company/intelligence.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/operatingmodel.py: real first-party scheduled chain acquires external evidence, proposes and accepts future objectives, persists revisions and applies them to ongoing role/domain operating capability. - External distinction: CompanyIntelligenceService.acquireavailableevidence and researchmodelunknowns create versioned company reality/claim inputs using supported ERPNext and public-source evidence; they are separate from current task execution. - Future / prospective distinction: LLM proposes strategic objectives with targets, KPI bindings and reversible experiments for future operating conditions; not just a retry of a failed task. - Adaptation option generated: New objective/KPI/experiment revisions proposed by LLM, validated against observed claims and source hash, subject to Observer acceptance or owner-review gate. - Path back into current capability / S3: Accepted revisions are persisted and included in OperatingModelLifecycleService.synthesize; subsequent reconcile/convergerolesandmandates updates domain state and role authority, then continuing WorkPortfolio domain loops execute work in the new current system. Owner S3=P can independently pause/release affected domains.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party closed ultimate identity or constitutional-policy decision despite legitimate human owner controls, global AutonomyPolicy thresholds, default business objectives, approval APIs and FOSS-only resource rules. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The company may change mission/purpose, legal owner responsibilities, financial exposure thresholds, acceptable risk and external-service legitimacy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Owner-authenticated APIs can set operational domain states, approve actions, update strategy/objective targets and change executive resource/mode thresholds; however no first-party identity/charter issue → legitimate ultimate company purpose judgment → binding institution-wide constitution/identity return path is demonstrated as distinct from current S3 interventions or future S4 portfolio revisions. - Decision owner: External legitimate human owner defines overarching company mission, deployment permissions, policy settings and company model; first-party services enforce changed operational rules, not a shipped S5 constitutional governance function with its own decision record. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ExecutiveCompanyOSService.updatepolicy/replaceobjectives, role approval/authorization gates, company owner console/API, default guardrails and FOSS resource inventory, domain mandates. - Closure path: Owner or developer config → updated current operating thresholds, company KPI/objective data or action approval → authorized work. No explicit S5 identity settlement/mission/purpose charter decision record with binding next-cycle internal policy above S3/S4; these are operation/strategy configuration tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Legitimate owner is present and S3=P is proven, but an owner editing defaults or approving a tool is not automatically S5=P. The functions and their decision/return must be distinguished at the organizational identity layer. - Evidence: backend/src/cyberteam/operations/executive.py; backend/src/cyberteam/api/routes/operations.py; backend/src/cyberteam/authorization/service.py; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/actionpolicy.py; README.md; backend/src/cyberteam/operations/operatingmodel.py. - Basis: Bounded no-evidence at first-party ultimate-policy layer after inspecting strongest parent pathways. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: A separate corporate constitution/owner mandate could exist in real deployment. This source review establishes concrete S3 parent domain powers, not a distinct source-backed S5 constitutional decision procedure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/96319ae4f89049b559aebcd43cebf5534fa332a2/assessments/cy/cyber-ai-team.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 96319ae4f89049b559aebcd43cebf5534fa332a2\">96319ae &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-04T18:29:24+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/christianhelle/puny\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Puny</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-04\">2026-10-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/christianhelle/puny/tree/dcdecb253b81415152aac4d3590d2186adfa0c81\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dcdecb253b81415152aac4d3590d2186adfa0c81\">dcdecb2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform coding work against the local project by interpreting a user task, selecting built-in repository/tool actions, changing source and observing results until the requested outcome is reached. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous codebases and tasks, incomplete local context, tool/provider failures, command/test results, Git state, file contents, context-window pressure and reviewer findings during orchestrated repair. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which project evidence/tool to use next, what source changes to make, how to react to tool/check results and when the coding task is complete. - Decision owner: the configured model-backed Puny coding actor operating through the first-party native chat/tool loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mode-specific tool registry; filesystem/shell/search/Git/web/skill tools; provider transports; session persistence; context compaction; retry handling; Git backstop commits; prompts and CLI/session lifecycle. - Closure path: task + repository/session state → model chooses tool/action → Puny executes against the environment → concrete result/error returns into the conversation → model revises the work or completes. - Boundary reachability: the normal supported CLI directly instantiates this model/tool loop with local or hosted provider choices; no external agent framework must supply the operational loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: if the model actor is removed while deterministic tools/session storage remain, open-ended coding judgment about what to inspect, change and do next disappears. - Evidence: src/main.zig; src/chat/chat.zig; src/chat/session.zig; src/tools/root.zig; README. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model inference is provider-supplied, but provider transport is a dependency; Puny concretely instantiates and governs the actor/tool feedback loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party coordination function among multiple same-recursion production S1 units was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Puny intentionally ships without subagents or first-party parallel worker organization; README directs users who want parallel work to run another independent instance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: single-session mode state; Git branch scope; one orchestrate phase at a time; provider/session identifiers. - Closure path: no distinct production-S1 interference → coordination response → changed peer behavior path exists in the shipped organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: fresh implement/review/fix contexts are sequential roles around one operation, while the reviewer is an audit role rather than a peer production S1. Independently launched Puny processes are not coordinated by Puny. - Evidence: README; src/chat/orchestrate.zig; src/tools/root.zig. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: plurality of phases/models is not sufficient for S2 without multiple operational S1s and a concrete interference relation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct whole-system current-control function over a portfolio of operational S1 commitments/resources was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: orchestrate iteration bounds, phase transitions, session state, context budgets and review outcomes regulate one focal coding operation rather than several operational units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S3 level. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: orchestrate state progression; max iterations; write blocking by mode; session/context budgets; Git preflight/backstop commits; retry/error handling. - Closure path: these mechanisms bound or sequence the current task; no whole-system current view plus substantive resource/priority/commitment intervention across multiple S1s exists. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the orchestrate controller is deterministic phase machinery. It enforces the implement/review/fix protocol but does not exercise discretionary current management over an operational portfolio. - Evidence: src/chat/orchestrate.zig; src/core/session.zig; src/chat/session.zig; src/review/review.zig. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a strong autonomous workflow controller is not automatically VSM S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently audit the committed branch produced by the coding actor, determine whether it is safe/complete enough to merge, and return blocking findings into a corrective implementation/re-review loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: implementation defects, regressions, compatibility/security/performance/error-handling problems, missing tests/docs, failed checks and false confidence by the implementing model. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a fresh model-backed reviewer independently decides whether evidence is complete and whether the immutable committed branch range is merge-worthy, while identifying concrete findings and recommended fixes. - Decision owner: the separate fresh-context Puny review actor. Deterministic report validation, write blocking and outcome conversion enforce the review contract but do not supply the substantive audit judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: beginPhase(.review) context reset; dedicated review system prompt; freshly fetched origin/main; immutable merge-base..HEAD scope; review-only read/check tool registry; global source-write block; savereviewresults; host validation of required sections/evidence flags; bounded orchestrate iterations. - Closure path: build actor commits implementation → Puny resets context and starts read-only reviewer over immutable committed range → reviewer directly inspects changed/surrounding files and can run relevant checks → reviewer saves evidence + merge-worthiness verdict → rejection writes review-results.md → fresh fix actor is explicitly instructed to open the report and resolve every finding → fixes are committed → another fresh review runs; approval terminates the loop as merge-worthy. - Boundary reachability: both /review/--review and /orchestrate/--orchestrate are shipped first-party CLI modes. Orchestrate directly wires the reviewer verdict into repair/re-review without user intervention. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the review model while retaining report-format validation, immutable Git scope and read-only enforcement removes the open-ended judgment about what is wrong, what evidence to gather and whether the branch is merge-worthy. The deterministic host can only validate/store an already chosen verdict. - Evidence: src/chat/orchestrate.zig; src/review/review.zig; src/tools/root.zig; src/tools/review.zig; src/prompts/prompts.zig; README. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: implement, review and fix may use the same configured provider/model family, but the audit has a fresh conversation, distinct review-only prompt/tool authority, immutable committed evidence scope and source-write prohibition; independence is role/context/evidence separation rather than provider diversity. - Claim being audited: that the current committed feature branch is merge-worthy relative to the freshly resolved origin/main baseline. - Ordinary reporting path: the build actor edits/commits source and could otherwise finish after its own checks and final response. - Complementary access path: the fresh review actor receives the immutable branch/base scope, directly reads project evidence and surrounding code, and can run read-only build/test/lint/check commands rather than trusting the implementer&#39;s self-report. - Independence boundary: review starts after a context reset, excludes the implement/orchestrate build prompt, blocks source mutation, exposes a distinct tool allowlist and scopes its verdict to committed Git evidence. It can reject work the implementer considered complete. - Who acts on findings: a subsequent fresh build-mode model context receives the generated report path and explicit instruction to resolve every listed finding; the repaired branch is then re-submitted to another fresh review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material autonomous outside-and-future intelligence/adaptation loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: durable sessions, saved PRDs, skills, web fetch, context compaction and provider/model selection improve or preserve current work, but they do not autonomously redesign persistent Puny capability/strategy for future operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S4 level. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session message persistence/resume; planning artifacts; skills loading; web fetch; context compaction; provider/model picker; review-derived current-task repair. - Closure path: no environmental/future sensing → selected persistent adaptation → later-operation behavior change loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: retained conversation/project instructions and reusable user-authored skills are context/capability inputs, not an autonomous prospective self-adaptation process. - Evidence: README; src/chat/persistence.zig; src/chat/session.zig; src/skills/; provider/model selection surfaces. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Puny can fetch current web evidence during a task, but current-task research is not by itself S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material identity/ultimate-policy decision loop was established at the assessed runtime recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: mode restrictions, system prompts, tool allowlists, source-write blocks, provider configuration and commit discipline constrain execution but remain operating/safety/process policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying identity-level conflict/proposal is decided by a legitimate ultimate authority and returned as binding organizational governance. - Decision owner: not established at S5 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: build/planning/review modes; static prompts; tool registries; write-block flag; CLI/config choices; Git branch preconditions; commit rules. - Closure path: no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate authority decision → returned governance → changed subsequent operation loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coding/review actors operate within preconfigured host rules; they do not own or resolve the system&#39;s identity/ultimate policy. - Evidence: src/prompts/prompts.zig; src/tools/root.zig; src/core/session.zig; CLI/configuration surfaces. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: review veto and write blocking are strong governance mechanisms, but they govern artifact progression rather than a genuine S5 policy/identity question.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6d8b2b615d62912d550a7a142eadcfc6acd32058/assessments/puny.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6d8b2b615d62912d550a7a142eadcfc6acd32058\">6d8b2b6 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-22T02:26:06+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/wu1w/tevarn\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tevarn</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-22\">2026-09-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/wu1w/tevarn/tree/e5e8e204ca2baf8ff671104ab6bf5529d40213ef\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e5e8e204ca2baf8ff671104ab6bf5529d40213ef\">e5e8e20 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously execute owner-directed work through model-selected tool use and, when delegated, through persistent employee agent jobs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user tasks, repository/file state, shell/browser/web/data observations, tool errors, capability limits, model/context uncertainty and job-specific evidence requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select substantive next actions/tools, interpret returned observations and decide what operational result is sufficient to return for the assigned work. - Decision owner: the running Tevarn model actor for the active session or persistent employee job. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: NexusAgentLoop, tool registry/executors, kernel process/token enforcement, permission/capability gates, context persistence, task grounding, completion gates and workforce inbox dispatch. - Closure path: owner/steward instruction → model decision → first-party tool execution → observation/evidence → subsequent model decision → final result persisted/returned; durable employee results return through workforce status/results to the steward organization. - Boundary reachability: the shipped NexusAgentLoop directly implements the model/tool feedback loop, and workforce jobs are dispatched into the same first-party runtime through the standard Identity + Inbox path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime machinery constrains and executes actions, but the model chooses the substantive course of work and synthesizes the outcome rather than following a fixed workflow result. - Evidence: backend/agent/loop.py, backend/agent/workforcedispatch.py, backend/tools/builtins/agentopstools.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external model providers host inference but do not inherit Tevarn organizational ownership; tool/runtime gates can terminate or constrain a run without becoming the S1 decision owner.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2-specific coordination path is established at the reviewed organization boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the review looked for concrete oscillation, collision or interference among distinct Tevarn employee S1 units rather than treating delegation, inbox queues, dependency ordering or global concurrency limiting as coordination by name. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party autonomous or constructor decision path was established that specifically selects/revises a coordination response to an evidenced inter-S1 disturbance. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workforce inbox queues, cluster dependency ordering/semaphores and kernel run gating can order or limit execution, but the reviewed evidence does not tie them to a reconstructable S2-specific mutual-adjustment decision path among operational units. - Closure path: no qualifying inter-S1 disturbance → coordination judgment/response → changed later S1 behavior loop was established beyond generic queueing/scheduling/enforcement. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the steward decomposes and assigns work, but task allocation alone is not S2; the runtime can serialize or cap execution, but deterministic enforcement alone does not establish the required coordination discretion. - Evidence: backend/agent/workforcedispatch.py, backend/agent/clusterexecutor.py, backend/agent/loop.py run-gate path. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Tevarn clearly supports multiple operational employees; this negative result is about the stricter S2 witness, not absence of multi-agent execution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current Tevarn organization by allocating work, budgets and capabilities, monitoring workforce state, intervening on failures/escalations and consolidating the current effort for the owner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: oversized/complex work, employee capability gaps, pending grants, budget exhaustion, failed/dead jobs, uneven work allocation, incomplete results and current organizational commitments that require intervention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether to execute directly or use the workforce; split complex work; select employees; assign/reassign jobs; set or top up budgets; approve task-relevant capability escalation; react to failed/budget-exhausted work; gather results and determine the next current-control intervention. - Decision owner: the model-driven CEO/steward actor in the first-party steward contact mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: crewsteward list/hire/assign/status/results/pendinggrants/grantcaps/setbudget/budgets/topup operations, workforce inbox persistence/dispatcher, kernel capability tokens, deterministic token accounting and budget hard stops. - Closure path: current workforce/job/budget/grant state → steward model judgment → assignment/resource/capability/recovery action → persisted inbox/identity/kernel state changes → employees run under the changed state → status/results return for the next steward judgment. - Boundary reachability: workforcedispatch.py injects the steward organizational contract into supported steward contacts, and crewsteward exposes the corresponding first-party current-control actions directly in the shipped runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the steward contract assigns current organizational choices to the model actor; budget/capability/run machinery enforces the selected constraints but does not decide which worker, budget change, grant or recovery action should be used. - Evidence: backend/agent/workforcedispatch.py, backend/tools/builtins/crewstewardtools.py, backend/agent/workforcebudget.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: simple task delegation alone is not used for this mapping; the positive state depends on the broader whole-workforce status/resource/escalation loop and enforceable intervention rights. - Whole-system current view: the steward can inspect the active workforce, per-employee identity/capabilities, queued/running job status/results, pending grants and budget/usage state across the organization it is managing. - Current-control decision scope: current work allocation, employee participation, per-job/persistent token budgets, capability escalation, requeue/recovery decisions and consolidation of current organizational outcomes.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary audit path is established for the assessed Tevarn organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect or weak operational claims were inspected through grounding and cluster-review mechanisms, but the available paths do not establish complementary access to operational reality independent enough to qualify as S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying independent audit owner/path was established that can inspect materially different evidence from the ordinary production/reporting path and feed findings back into control. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: taskgrounding.py requires evidence/tool usage for hallucination-prone task classes; cluster review can parse returned deliverables and ask a reviewer model for verdicts; completion gates can require further work. These are useful verification controls but do not by themselves supply independent operational access. - Closure path: grounding/review can nudge, revise or reject a response, but the reviewed evidence did not establish an independent audit channel with materially different access to the underlying operational reality. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-based reviewer judgment may be agentic, but reviewer naming or a second model is insufficient when its access remains the produced deliverable/ordinary evidence path rather than an independent complementary audit surface. - Evidence: backend/agent/taskgrounding.py, backend/agent/clusterexecutor.py, completion-gate surfaces in backend/agent/. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a future mode that gives a distinct reviewer direct raw-artifact/replay/probe access could change this classification without changing S1/S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-then adaptation loop is established at the reviewed boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing external conditions, future opportunities/threats and learned owner preferences were inspected, but no shipped path closes sensing → prospective option generation → adaptation of present organizational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no autonomous or constructor owner/path was established that turns external/future distinctions into adaptation options and returns a chosen option into current S3/capability. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web/browser tools can sense the environment; memory services can persist information; autoremember.py heuristically stores selected chat decisions/history; skills/MCP/configuration can change capability when explicitly operated. - Closure path: no qualifying environmental/future distinction → generated adaptation option → returned capability/current-control change loop is supplied by the standard distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: autoremember.py explicitly performs no LLM call and uses regex/length rules to store notes; memory persistence or available web search therefore does not establish prospective agent-owned adaptation. - Evidence: backend/agent/autoremember.py, reviewed memory/tool/skill surfaces and steward tooling. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an ordinary task may research future facts, but task-local research is not credited as organization-level S4 unless it develops and closes a prospective adaptation option into present capability.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: preserve ultimate purpose/direction by reserving strategic ambiguity and project-level direction decisions to the legitimate owner above the steward organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguity over strategic direction, owner intent, project-level commitments or decisions whose consequences should not be settled by the steward as routine current control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: answer strategy-level clarification and set/approve the direction or project decision that the steward must then operationalize. - Decision owner: the human owner as legitimate parent authority for the assessed personal-agent organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the steward role contract explicitly separates owner vs CEO responsibilities; first-party clarify can block and return an owner answer; ordinary session state then carries the returned instruction into subsequent model/tool/workforce decisions. - Closure path: strategic/identity-level ambiguity encountered by steward → clarify/owner conversation reaches the owner → owner decides direction/project point → answer returns into the active session → steward decomposes/assigns/acts under that returned direction. - Boundary reachability: owner contact is part of the ordinary supported personal-Agent-OS boundary, the steward contract explicitly names the owner as direction/project decision authority, and the shipped clarify tool provides the runtime return channel. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no first-party internal ultimate-authority mode was established; the CEO/steward is explicitly subordinate on direction/strategic clarification while retaining current S3 authority, so the qualifying S5 closure is parent-governed rather than autonomous. - Evidence: backend/agent/workforcedispatch.py, backend/tools/builtins/agentopstools.py (clarify). - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: ordinary permission confirmation, task approval or capability escalation is not counted as S5; the positive mapping is limited to the explicit owner-reserved direction/strategy path. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: the organization&#39;s owner-directed purpose/strategic direction and project-level choices that define what the steward organization should pursue rather than how to execute current work. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: parent mode only — the human owner; no first-party autonomous or constructor S5 mode is established at this boundary. - Return-to-operation path: the owner answer returns through the active owner/steward session, after which the steward&#39;s model/tool/workforce decisions are made under the clarified direction.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/69ab172a63ce5eacb442e6d85020ca9232eae676/assessments/tevarn.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 69ab172a63ce5eacb442e6d85020ca9232eae676\">69ab172 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T21:05:50+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/karlorz/atomcode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AtomCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/karlorz/atomcode/tree/152b2cc132dda9b177c3a030e21cfacd95494ea3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 152b2cc132dda9b177c3a030e21cfacd95494ea3\">152b2cc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform and verify software changes through the model/tool/feedback loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user requests, repository contents, compiler/test failures, tool results, budget/goal conditions, provider output and permissions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next coding tool/action, revise a patch based on feedback, invoke review, and decide task completion. - Decision owner: main model-backed coding agent (and bounded child coding models for delegated file tasks). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: kernel Agent and TurnRunner, coding tool registry, permissions, turn budgets, session, goal evaluator, post-edit verification, notifications. - Closure path: prompt → model-chosen code/tool action → first-party tool execution → returned observation → further model reasoning/action or final response. - Boundary reachability: the standard coding-agent assembly builds this loop and registers first-party tools for interactive/headless use. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime enforces scope, timeouts and dispatch, but the model retains the task-specific code operation choices. - Evidence: kernel agent, coding assembly, core loop. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external model/provider availability is a dependency rather than a credited system owner.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no bounded first-party inter-S1 interference-specific coordination/feedback loop is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel child edits could conflict over a shared repository, but deterministic per-file task partition, queueing and concurrency caps do not show incompatible-change detection/attenuation with returned child correction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct S2-specific arbitration right established. - Decision owner: not established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SubAgentPool concurrent JoinSet, per-file SubAgentTask, timeout/retry limits, child tool filtering, progress/result events. - Closure path: task results return to the main coding model, but no specific inter-S1 conflicting-operation attenuation/return relationship was reconstructed. - Boundary reachability: no positive S2 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: selecting one file per child reduces a possible overlap by design, but this is decomposition/serialization; it is not a demonstrated organizational S2 controller that senses interaction disturbances and changes subsequent S1 work. - Evidence: paralleledit.rs, background.rs. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: does not mean agents can never be assembled into an S2-capable multi-team configuration; no unsupported cross-agent owner imported here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct current whole-system manager owning resource/commitment discretion across a live set of S1 units was reconstructed from the shipped modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: goal progress, turn caps and individual background sessions are tracked, but this is operation-level control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate whole-current portfolio/priority/commitment decision over multiple S1 units. - Decision owner: not established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: goal-evaluation continuation, turn-level progress/budget guard, per-file subagent pool, background task session control, CLI/TUI status. - Closure path: current coding work may continue or stop based on model feedback and deterministic budgets, without a separate S3 current supervisory return. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: goal-mode agents and deterministic background schedulers act on bounded coding tasks; they do not automatically own overall current organizational commitments. - Evidence: goal evaluator, goal lifecycle, subagent pool, background agent. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the model has real choice within individual tasks; that is credited S1, not general whole-current management.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: in the separately callable shipped codereview mode, independently inspect actual changed source and Git diff, judge implementation defects, and return structured findings for coding-agent correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: divergence between an implementer&#39;s reported success and defects in actual changed code, APIs, correctness/security/reliability implications. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently decide/report defect findings from a direct evidence path, not merely relay implementing agent&#39;s own tool outcomes. - Decision owner: distinct read-only reviewer Agent with a dedicated persona, message context and tool set; the implementing main model owns follow-up corrective code operations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: first-party ReviewTool, live git diff, diff line annotations and impact/risk plan, review-only registry, path confinement, structured reportfinding sink, change-scope filter and findings sorting, provider reuse. - Closure path: parent/host model invokes codereview → tool obtains Git diff from LIVE workspace → separate reviewer inspects surrounding source, reports findings → tool returns structured evidence to host model → host can revise code and re-run checks/review. - Boundary reachability: shipped PrepareOptions::default().review=true, parts.rs registers ReviewTool in coding agent tool registry, fills its shared provider on assembly; the codereview tool then instantiates a fresh restricted read-only reviewer with direct repository access. No custom extension or benchmark-only setup is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the separate reviewer model determines actual defect judgments, while Git diff retrieval, access confinement and rendering are deterministic supports; a common provider does not share agent history or mutable authority. - Claim being audited: actual worktree/staged/base code changes relative to the implementation task, not only the main agent&#39;s self-report. - Ordinary reporting path: main coding-agent messages, tool results, tests and same-agent post-edit verification. - Complementary access path: independent reviewer starts from live Git diff, directly reads/greps/inspects source via its restricted tool registry, and writes structured reportfinding judgments. - Independence boundary: fresh read-only reviewer agent with own persona/messages and no write/edit/bash tools, path-confined to repo; possible shared LLM provider/model family is a dependency and not a common decision context. - Who acts on findings: invoking primary coding model receives prioritized results and may modify code or request more tests; review tool itself remains read-only. - Evidence: ReviewTool, read-only child build, coding mount, path guard. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: available as a separately triggered tool; not necessarily executed on every coding turn. The source confirms independence of reviewer context/tool authority, not that every reviewer verdict is correct. Ordinary same-agent verification by verify.rs is expressly excluded as the positive audit witness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party prospective external environment-intelligence loop culminating in adaptation of the organization&#39;s future capability was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current requests, provider/tool outcomes and stored past session information are available, but no distinct outside-and-then intelligence function is shown. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no future organizational capability adaptation owner proven. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent sessions, memory/context, web tools, code graphs, goal mode, skills, provider/model selection. - Closure path: current task information may inform subsequent tasks, but an external future signal → adaptation option → model/parent-owned capability change → returned altered operation was not established. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agentic browsing and persistent context can support immediate coding operations; their existence does not establish strategic external intelligence ownership. - Evidence: coding assembly, goal evaluator, README feature boundary. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: possible user-defined future extensions not bundled/wired in the reviewed mode cannot be credited.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no constitutional identity/purpose reconciliation loop with an ultimate first-party decision owner and authoritative return was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool risk, task objectives and provider configuration are enforcement and operational constraints, not a whole-system identity or ultimate purpose matter. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none for organization-level ultimate policy/identity in the declared boundary. - Decision owner: none established for S5; user authorization does not automatically create an S5 parent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system prompt, tool permission policies, sensitive path checks, approval screens, provider settings, remote access/session controls. - Closure path: a user may allow or deny an edit or switch a model, but there is no declared identity-policy matter → legitimate ultimate authority → binding returned policy/identity decision loop. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves fixed permissions, not a model-owned ultimate organizational charter. - Evidence: coding assembly, README safety/modes. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: upstream human product/OSS governance is an adjacent actor unless a supported runtime loop binds it as an S5 parent decision right.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/80fc101edd018461ab3b14a5b707af6977cda275/assessments/atomcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 80fc101edd018461ab3b14a5b707af6977cda275\">80fc101 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T04:06:20+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/codemonkeying/lean-coder\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">lean-coder</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/codemonkeying/lean-coder/tree/f143f85344c0b0e5b3dcb68b5a1f884ac1c5ec63\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f143f85344c0b0e5b3dcb68b5a1f884ac1c5ec63\">f143f85 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform context-sensitive coding through model-selected file reading, editing, search and shell actions with actual execution feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown source layout, failed edits/commands, dependency/test failures and context pressure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Active root/worker coding model picks next tool action from observed real output. - Decision owner: Native lean-coder agent using provider inference in the integrated model/tool feedback loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider adapter, small tool registry, confirmation leash, session history/compaction and worker execution limits. - Closure path: User goal → model-selected action → file/shell/tool result → model receives result → next action or reply. - Boundary reachability: Supported leancoder executable drives coding tools and optional model-backed dispatched workers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Permissions and tool registry alone cannot select appropriate coding steps; context-dependent choices require the active model. - Evidence: main loop; tools; standard modes. - Basis: structural and explicit -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate concurrent edit interference when two real S1 worker processes target the same repository file. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two workers selecting the same path for edits could overwrite each other&#39;s uncommitted work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: First-claim-wins append-only path claim log deterministically identifies one live owner with TTL; second claimant receives an explicit REFUSED and instruction to choose other work or wait. - Decision owner: Constructor-written file claim resolution; no agent chooses the arbitration policy dynamically, hence C rather than A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session-scoped claims.jsonl, atomic append-order reduction, TTL/release and dispatchworker boardclaim/boardrelease/boardlist tools. - Closure path: Two child S1s request same file → claim log resolves live winner → loser is REFUSED and instructed not to edit → next worker tool choice can avoid collision. - Boundary reachability: Optional first-party dispatchworker provides file claim tools to actually executing worker subprocesses and routes them to core claim reducers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Constraint is deterministic and model obeys conflict signal; a model is not responsible for selecting the winner or negotiating an alternate policy. - Distinct S1 units: Separate worker agents executing under shared driver run with their own provider turns and file tools. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concurrent edits to the same project file. - Attenuating coordination relation: First-live-claim file reservation with conflict rejection and timeout/release. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Loser receives REFUSED with holder and must choose different file/work or wait, avoiding same-path edit attempt. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The explicit claim tool detects a concrete contested path, refuses a conflicting operation and communicates a changed action constraint, rather than just queueing unrelated tasks. - Evidence: boardclaim and reducer; workerboard tools. - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Manage active worker commitments and current organization-level assignment readiness across a bounded coding team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple delegated workers and dependencies create unfinished, blocked, failed and ready work that requires reassignment/integration and active notifications. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Root driver model chooses create/add/assign/cancel, reads board readiness/work results and responds to completion/dead-branch notifications, potentially dispatching/resuming a peer for changed current commitments. - Decision owner: Model-driven driver acting over worker S1s; deterministic locks, notifications and DAG status enforcement are supporting control plane machinery. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Driver-only task board interface, atomic taskboardmutate, persisted task DAG/assignee/assignedby, peer status and wake, result reconcile, worker dispatch. - Closure path: Board current task/worker view → root agent chooses worker and ready work, pushes assignment → worker acts and marks outcome → assigner woken with results/dead dependents → root model updates later tasks/assignments. - Boundary reachability: Opt-in first-party board and worker plugins are loaded by the supported CLI; the driver is the model owner of create/assign/cancel and can reach actual worker processes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Root model exercises current multi-worker assignment and repair discretion; board rule enforcement alone would not pick work or priorities. - Whole-system current view: Named board lists all task statuses/assignees, dependencies and peer liveness; /worker status and board result are available for the present run. - Current-control decision scope: Create/update current commitments, assign to running/dormant worker, cancel, detect failing blocked branch and reconcile actual subtask outputs. - Evidence: board driver actions and return notifications; taskboardmutate/ready; workers and resume. - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No integrated complementary reality-sampling audit authority returning findings to whole-current management is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Wrong code or incomplete worker reports can be checked by routine tests, code review user requests or a delegated reviewer, but none is first-party required independent operational audit wiring. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Root model/worker can inspect source and request another agent; no separate sampled evidence and audit judgment closure is established by the default or enabled worker contracts. - Decision owner: Ordinary coding S1/manager or user for ad-hoc review; no distinct S3 decision owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Worker transcript/result pages, named review role, git summary and diagnostics, demo audit. - Closure path: Optional review tool result returns to requester like any ordinary delegated S1 result, without independent auditor requirement → S3 control return contract. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A label review, a separate model instance, or routine code/test tools are insufficient to infer a complementary independent audit. - Evidence: worker contract; worker status/result; coding demo. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party future/environment-facing intelligence and capability-adoption loop connected back to current-team control is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider and tooling options, remote SSH and compaction can change, but these are operator-selected capabilities or current-task context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator installs/enables providers/tools; model acts on present coding requests rather than a prospective organizational adaptation remit. - Decision owner: Human for capability configuration, ordinary coding agent for local task decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Providers, optional update tool, web search, code/project context compaction. - Closure path: Operator changes provider/tool → coding agent executes with newly configured resource. Missing future environment scan, option evaluation and adoption decision returning into S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Context summarization, present-task research and tool installation do not by themselves establish S4. - Evidence: provider/runtime; provider/update model; update optional tool. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime ultimate organizational identity/constitutional policy arbiter returns binding decisions into the whole continuing worker organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Leash modes, approval settings and worker-depth constraints control tool safety and recursion, not ultimate collective purpose. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator approves commands or edits and sets tool availability; deterministic software applies them. - Decision owner: Human for permissions/tool configuration; runtime for enforcement, not S5 ultimate policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Leash levels chat/r/rw/rwe, ask/session/auto approvals, tool capability ceiling, security controls, driver-only board operations. - Closure path: User configuration/permission → individual tool allowed/denied, not identity-level dispute → legitimate decision → organization policy return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tool access and prompt role labels do not confer ultimate constitutional authority. - Evidence: Safety section; permissions; driver-only role controls. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3e2b96646ce33a6d905808b7411e27f1210ec9ac/assessments/lean-coder.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3e2b96646ce33a6d905808b7411e27f1210ec9ac\">3e2b966 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-05T03:47:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mars01pi/marspi-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Marspi CLI</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-05\">2026-10-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mars01pi/marspi-cli/tree/e8e46a39e9da2dac1444c175ad241f2cfa71c7dc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e8e46a39e9da2dac1444c175ad241f2cfa71c7dc\">e8e46a3 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform coding and repository work through a model-driven tool feedback loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous codebases, incomplete context, file/search/shell outcomes, model outputs, tool failures, context limits, user goals and workspace safety constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next investigation/edit/tool action, revise work from returned evidence, and decide when the requested coding result is complete. - Decision owner: the active model-backed coding actor instantiated through the first-party runner composition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent.Runner, tool registry, context manager and persistence, prompt assembly, Smart Routing/provider adapter, memory/skill managers, TUI/plain session loop and user cancellation/confirmation surfaces. - Closure path: user goal + session context → model chooses a coding/tool action → tool/runtime returns repository or execution evidence → evidence re-enters the model context → the actor chooses the next action until completion or bounded stop. - Boundary reachability: ordinary TUI and plain modes call a.runner.Loop directly; /loop also instantiates repeated model-backed operational contexts through the same first-party runner interface. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor leaves routing, persistence, tools and UI but removes open-ended coding decisions; deterministic/runtime components transport and constrain those decisions. - Evidence: README; cmd/root.go; cmd/repl.go; cmd/engine.go; go.mod. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the underlying runner implementation was split into the first-party sibling marspi-core and is not revision-pinned by this CLI ref; the positive finding relies on the frozen CLI&#39;s direct invocation contract plus its documented ReAct operating role, not on unpinned sibling internals.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path establishes attenuation of a specific interference or oscillation among distinct S1 coding units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Loop Engineering sequences implementer, verifier and updater roles; Supervisor/DevFlow wrappers dispatch or sequence work and persist graph state, but the frozen CLI does not expose a concrete cross-S1 collision relation being damped. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific coordination decision over peer interference was established. - Decision owner: not established at S2 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fixed Loop Engineering sequencing, graph invocation, worker lists, checkpointing, HITL callbacks and workflow phase order. - Closure path: these mechanisms route/delegate/audit work, but no evidenced coordination result specifically changes later S1 behavior to attenuate an identified inter-S1 disturbance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: multiple roles and a star topology do not by themselves establish S2. - Evidence: README; cmd/engine.go; cmd/enginegraph.go; cmd/enginesupervisor.go; cmd/enginedevflow.go; go.mod. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: unpinned marspi-graph internals are not used to infer hidden conflict-management behavior.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: ? - Function: a plausible whole-current supervisory path exists through the experimental star-topology Supervisor, but the frozen CLI does not expose enough of its decisive algorithm to establish or defensibly exclude S3. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the wrapper supplies a goal, worker roster, max steps, checkpoints, HITL gates and a result carrying lastagent; the hidden sibling orchestrator may regulate current worker commitments, but that cannot be reconstructed from the CLI ref alone. - Decisive decision or feedback right: unresolved — the frozen wrapper does not show what state the supervisor model observes when choosing workers, what current resource/priority/commitment discretion it owns, or whether routing is only task decomposition. - Decision owner: unresolved between a model-backed supervisor path and deterministic graph orchestration because marspi-graph is locally replaced but not revision-pinned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SupervisorConfig, fixed worker specs, MaxSteps, coder HITL approval, SQLite checkpointer, resume/list UI and cancellation. - Closure path: the wrapper shows supervisor invocation and returned graph state, but does not expose enough of the internal decision→worker→updated-whole-state loop to prove the Profile&#39;s S3 closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a component named Supervisor and dynamic worker handoff are insufficient; ownership remains unresolved without the pinned decision path. - Evidence: README; cmd/enginesupervisor.go; cmd/enginesupervisortest.go; go.mod. - Basis: explicit wrapper evidence + unresolved revision provenance. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: use ?, not —, because a concrete first-party Supervisor integration is reachable and plausibly material, while the dependency revision needed to decide its organizational semantics is absent from the frozen CLI.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge an Implementer&#39;s code claim by direct artifact inspection and tests, then feed failures into autonomous rework. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the implementing actor may produce code that appears complete but fails tests, misses the goal or contains logic defects that its own self-report does not reveal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect changed files, select and run the appropriate test command, judge PASS/FAIL from actual test evidence and goal coverage, and return a failure diagnosis when needed. - Decision owner: the separate model-backed Verifier context. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh verifier agentctx.Manager, changed-file extraction, read/grep/bash tools through the runner, explicit verifier prompt, deterministic loop sequencing, separate Updater context and iteration cap. - Closure path: Implementer changes code and reports completion → fresh Verifier directly inspects changed artifacts and runs tests → PASS terminates the loop; FAIL → fresh Updater analyzes verifier evidence and generates a refined prompt → prompt is appended to the Implementer context → next implementation iteration → a fresh Verifier audits again. - Boundary reachability: /loop &lt;goal&gt; is a shipped CLI command implemented directly in cmd/engine.go; no external reviewer product or custom composition is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the verifier model leaves deterministic sequencing but removes the independent semantic PASS/FAIL judgment and diagnosis; the audit decision is therefore agent-owned. - Evidence: README; cmd/engine.go; cmd/root.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the verifier uses the same provider/runner infrastructure as other roles, but it has a fresh context, a distinct role contract, direct repository/test access, and an explicit closed rework path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no externally and prospectively oriented adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Smart Routing, long-term memory, web search, skills and context compaction can change how the current coding task is handled. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party path was found that models future/external change, develops adaptation options for the harness and returns a selected option into present organizational capability. - Decision owner: not established at S4 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: routing scores/model tier selection, memory search/append, skills, web search, context compaction and provider configuration. - Closure path: these mechanisms serve current-task execution or retain current experience; they do not close an outside-and-then adaptation conversation with current-control capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory, task research and model routing are not S4 without a future/external option-generation loop. - Evidence: README; cmd/root.go; provider/routing interfaces visible through the frozen composition. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: project-development evolution and sibling-repository roadmap work are adjacent and excluded.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity- or ultimate-policy-level closure is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dangerous-command confirmation, workspace path policy, coder handoff approval, push approval, model/provider configuration and user cancellation bound ordinary execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: users may approve operational coder handoffs or pushes, but no identity/ultimate-policy issue is routed to a legitimate authority and returned as a durable identity decision for the system. - Decision owner: not established at S5 level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: confirmation UI, outside-read/write policy, Supervisor RequireApprovalFor, DevFlow push HITL, environment/config flags and stop controls. - Closure path: confirmations change specific current actions; they do not close identity/ultimate-policy questions for subsequent operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime safety and HITL gates constrain S1/workflow actions but do not constitute S5. - Evidence: README; cmd/enginesupervisor.go; cmd/enginedevflow.go; cmd/repl.go. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: operator and maintainer authority exists, but the frozen operating boundary does not expose an identity-level return loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6d909a3429120d554b402f1ec46a620b9377f59e/assessments/marspi-cli.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6d909a3429120d554b402f1ec46a620b9377f59e\">6d909a3 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-17T20:41:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aipoch/open-science\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AIPOCH Open-Science</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-17\">2026-10-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aipoch/open-science/tree/b89d0e134ad65e197c2cefddda69921a5458a552\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b89d0e134ad65e197c2cefddda69921a5458a552\">b89d0e1 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce distinct research task outputs in user project environments through active, model-directed autonomous ACP scientific/coding sessions launched and supervised by AIPOCH. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Ambiguous scientific questions, changing local literature/data, code/execution results, permission requests, tool errors and subsequent user clarification. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The configured autonomous external ACP agent (Claude/OpenCode/Codex or compatible supported target) chooses task-local model/tool actions, responds to tool and Notebook/connector results and produces a final research report/artifact while AIPOCH owns agent-session admission and feedback/report transport. - Decision owner: External model-backed autonomous ACP agent actually launched by AIPOCH first-party supported product path. AIPOCH owns the containing local research-cell session and control-plane boundary, not the agent&#39;s implementation details. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskAgentPort, ACP session create/resume and prompt forwarding, streamed tool observation, sandbox/Notebook/connector bridge, permission broker, project artifact collection, failure/stop/restart controls. - Closure path: User project research goal → AIPOCH app creates configured ACP task Session and forwards prompt → model agent chooses scientific analysis/tool use → actual external/local tool observations return to provider agent → agent adjusts task actions/report → AIPOCH records outputs/provenance and later task continuation. - Boundary reachability: TaskRunner.startRun and executeRun call TaskAgentPort.prompt; shipped ACP adapter calls runtime.sendPromptObserved or sendPrompt on attached provider session. Desktop/headless CLI open-science run uses same service. No optional private plugin required for this app-owned session/actor relation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Substantive next-action choice occurs in the running autonomous ACP agent; without that dependency TaskRunner remains a session/permission/persistence controller, not a scientist. Ownership claim includes only the reachable S1 research work cell, not upstream agent-native subroles/plans. - Evidence: src/main/tasks/task-runner.ts; src/main/acp/task-agent-port.ts; src/main/acp/agent-process.ts; packages/open-science/CLI.md. - Basis: First-party source-attested launch/prompt/observation relation plus supported external autonomous agent as operational S1; internal agent semantics not directly reviewed. - Confidence: High for reachable autonomous operating relation; bounded on underlying external agent capabilities. - Caveats: Research agent&#39;s model/tool-selection loop is not authored inside AIPOCH; different configured backends may differ and external ACP actor must be installed/authenticated.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No source-demonstrated mutual disturbance regulation between distinct independently operating S1 scientific research sessions beyond persistence/duplicate-identity admission. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Overlapping runs could target the same Session identity or mutate the same persisted project metadata, creating conflicting transcript writes; the reviewed protection applies to identical Session or Project records rather than two interacting independent scientific task units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Session admission sessionbusy and operation-scheduler serialization guard same-identity persistence consistency; no source-backed inter-S1 contention assessment and coordinated sibling behavioral adjustment was found. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic reservation/persistence scheduler enforcing prewritten identity contracts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: activeRunBySession reservation and release, session identity scope tails, version/revision conflict guards, global mutation barrier, task progress ledger. - Closure path: Second start on same Session id is rejected or same-key metadata mutation awaits lock; no distinct operational sibling S1 activity observes a conflict and receives an S2 coordination response that changes its scientific operation. - Distinct S1 units checked: Two different ACP research sessions can exist across projects; same-session two prompt attempts instead address one operational identity. Same-file/project concurrent agents and inter-task resource arbitration weren&#39;t shown through these reviewed guards. - Inter-S1 disturbance candidate checked: Duplicate access to a live Session transcript and shared project storage; identity scheduling is about authoritative record consistency, not task-operational interference demonstrated between different ongoing agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A deterministic lock does not automatically make a constructor-owned S2 function. S2 requires a specific inter-S1 variety/feedback witness, not simply generic session locking. - Evidence: src/main/tasks/task-runner.ts; src/main/session-persistence/operation-scheduler.ts; src/main/session-persistence/coordinator.ts; src/main/session-persistence/operation-scheduler.test.ts. - Basis: Bounded negative after checking strongest actual admission/lock path. - Confidence: Medium-high for this installed organization boundary. - Caveats: These are genuine safety/correctness controls; a separate compute scheduling or workspace-conflict function could change S2 if it demonstrated a specific cross-session operational interference and response.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: A first-party current-project/session control plane over a live portfolio of research operations: select current tasks, configure model/provider and resources/permissions, start/resume/cancel active runs and inspect or intervene on their status. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Research project tasks may be active/failed/interrupted/stale, pending approval, require compute/permissions, need cancellation or a configuration/continuation decision, or risk incorrect session ownership. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Task API/Desktop/CLI display the whole local project/session run set and expose functions to create task sessions, continue/start research, modify selected compute hosts and permission/delegation profile, cancel/stop tasks, review outputs and resume. Operator owns which current work/limits to apply, while the first-party system handles approved changes and persists state. There is no packaged autonomous whole-of-project manager choosing those interventions by default. - Decision owner: Base constructor mode: function-specific TaskRunner/TaskAgent APIs that a developer could compose into an autonomous current-control actor; human/operator parent mode: Desktop/CLI/Task API user selects current work and intervenes from project/session/run status. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Durable projects/run journal, listProjects/listSessions/getRun/status/progress, startRun/cancelRun, session configuration revision checks, task admission constraints, compute host selection, approvals/permissions and agent pause/restart. - Closure path: Whole project/session current state → caller chooses start/resume/adjust active agent/compute/permission/cancel → TaskRunner applies command to ACP session and persists mutation/terminal state → displayed status, artifacts and subsequent task running state change. - Whole-system current view: All project records, sessions and active run identities with status/progress/outputs/selected compute/permission and provenance; not full provider-internal intentions or scientific quality. - Current-control decision scope: Multiple concurrent live research session commitments and their admission/current resources, continuation, cancellation and operator/parent-controlled configuration. - Boundary reachability: Packaged open-science run, run status, run cancel, session status/config update, local Task API and GUI use TaskRunner&#39;s real current-control methods; not a developer-only test surface. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The host exposes/enforces whole-current actions but no first-party autonomous manager/optimizer chooses the system-wide current work plan; C for composable manager path. A legitimate operator owns current decisions in the shipped parent console path, hence (P). Neither tool-permission approval alone nor startRun in isolation would establish this. - Evidence: src/main/tasks/task-runner.ts; src/main/acp/task-agent-port.ts; src/main/web-service/task-api.ts; packages/open-science/CLI.md; src/main/session-persistence/coordinator.ts. - Basis: Implemented current multi-session portfolio and command-path source plus user-facing control documentation. - Confidence: Medium-high on parent current-work control and constructor; no automatic dynamic priority negotiation or lab-wide business management. - Caveats: The parent human may not be present in all unattended modes; classification records supported alternatives, not simultaneous ownership.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Challenge autonomous research agent&#39;s outputs and scientific artifact claims with a fresh-context, separately model-directed, scope-limited complementary evidence-access path and return unresolved warnings into corrective activity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Agent conclusions may cite unsupported sources, conceal action failures, claim generated charts/data without reproducible trace, omit risk limitations, misread original files or report a flawed scientific task outcome. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently prompted Reviewer ACP model uses allowed read-only reviewer-specific evidence tools, checks the actual turn/artifact/trace and submits structured pass/warn/fail check findings rather than using the main agent&#39;s self-assessment. Fresh Reviewer judgment determines what to challenge. - Decision owner: A separate autonomous ACP Reviewer model invoked through first-party Reviewer architecture and its bounded MCP evidence bridge; the host decides lifecycle, scope, persistence and bounded correction orchestration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Turn-scope snapshot and digest, raw evidence resolver, immutable artifact version/provenance readers, read-only Reviewer MCP server, structured findings schema/IDs, reviewer ACP session lifecycle, correction prompt / fix-loop limits. - Closure path: Completed ordinary agent output and artifact claims → first-party scope-bounded independent Reviewer session reads alternative raw/provenance/trace evidence → Reviewer emits structured warn/fail/pass findings → host injects [Auditor] request to main session and re-reviews each correction up to limit → findings resolved or explicitly remain unaddressed. - Claim being audited: The produced scientific output, cited sources, tool execution correctness and artifact-specific provenance for a given completed run/turn. - Ordinary reporting path: Original ACP agent&#39;s answer/plan/tool events and published artifacts, persisted as session run result. - Complementary access path: Fresh ACP Reviewer session with separately scoped MCP readturn/real artifact files and provenance, test/trace evidence, external to original agent&#39;s own selective narrative; no ordinary write-tools granted to reviewer. - Independence boundary: Distinct session and controlled evidence interface with bounded rights, but same host process and potentially same upstream provider/model, not guaranteed separate security realm/human. - Who acts on findings: First-party runReviewWithSession detects warn/fail and calls runReviewerFixLoop, which sends correction to main agent; independent re-review tracks stable sourceFindingId and unresolved outcomes. - Boundary reachability: TaskRunner after a persisted completed agent output invokes reviewer.review if autoReviewEnabled === true; Reviewer IPC/manual run supports same path. runReviewAssessment creates actual Reviewer ACP session; runReviewerFixLoop sends correction and re-review to the main ACP session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The audit finding is selected by an independent model reading complementary evidence; deterministic storage, gates and correction scheduling do not make the finding only constructor-owned. Thus S3=A in enabled Reviewer mode. - Evidence: src/main/reviewer/orchestrator.ts; src/main/reviewer/review-assessment-owner.ts; src/main/reviewer/mcp-server.ts; src/main/reviewer/reviewer-fix-loop-owner.ts; src/main/reviewer/turn-evidence.ts; src/main/tasks/task-runner.ts. - Basis: Source-attested bounded separate model audit with real main-agent correction return. - Confidence: High for installed conditional correction wiring; no empirical scientific review accuracy tested. - Caveats: Reviewer auto-invocation is opt-in and appears default false for Task API; Reviewer errors do not invalidate an already completed task; fix loop limited to three rounds and can mark unresolved; evidence access is scoped and not guaranteed complete.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party owned externally/prospectively driven change of the research installation&#39;s future capabilities/options with selected return into S3 established at pinned boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing scientific data, tool catalog, external provider/model choices and skills could affect later work but current features are configurable/developer or vendor governed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator chooses current installed model, skill and connector; app can discover/pull updates and reuse artifact histories, but no demonstrated first-party actor systematically compares external future scenarios and selects a capability adaptation returned to active project control. - Decision owner: Vendor/developer/operator for offered dependencies, installed features and model/provider selection; first-party runtime transmits/applies configurations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Scientific connector catalogs, skill marketplace/install, model settings, reusable research history, artifact provenance, product update engine. - Closure path: External scientific search/current data → present S1 research tasks; configurable future model/skill availability → operator updates app manually. No S4-owned environmental scanning → prospective alternatives → measured choice → returned organization-wide changed capability cycle. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Scientific work about future research is S1&#39;s user task, not AIPOCH&#39;s own future ability to perform it. Installing a connector/model version is not evidence of institutional intelligence selection. - Evidence: README.md; src/main/tasks/task-runner.ts; src/main/acp/session-agent-target.ts; packages/open-science/CLI.md. - Basis: Bounded negative review of model/connector/skill and external data paths. - Confidence: Medium; first-party future adaptation decision could exist in additional capabilities outside inspected candidate paths. - Caveats: Does not deny model-provider choice, new data ingestion or update distribution; only their insufficiency to establish the specific organizational S4 function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party ultimate value/identity/ethos conflict settlement authority shown beyond permissions and prechosen local trust settings. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Sensitive local/private scientific data, untrusted tools and connector capabilities, network exposure, remote compute permissions, unsafe shell/edit action and credential handling. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Users choose permission mode, delegated task allow/deny, project model and tools; app enforces typed permissions, stores protected credentials and can cancel dangerous work. It does not adjudicate ultimate institutional policy goals or expose a legitimate S5 settlement loop. - Decision owner: Human research institution/operator for actual ethics/research identity outside the app, project developer for fixed security primitives. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Ask-for-approval/auto/full profile, trust-scoped grants, local secrets, reviewer permission restrictions, compute policy, project/session config and audit/provenance records. - Closure path: Preselected permission and trust rule → tool/run gate, task continues/pauses/rejects. There is no distinct values dispute → ultimate legitimate institutional decision → binding policy change returned through S3–S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Manual approving a notebook command is a safety/authorization step, not higher-level identity/mission governance; keeping sensitive data local does not create S5. - Evidence: README.md; src/main/acp/permission-profile-controller.ts; src/main/acp/permission-broker.ts; src/main/tasks/task-runner.ts. - Basis: Bounded negative of highest plausible policy/trust owners and their actual return paths. - Confidence: Medium-high at local first-party software boundary. - Caveats: A deploying laboratory or scientific ethics board might exercise genuine S5 outside the assessed AIPOCH installation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2ff0924f0e2ffdc85ce9d0f4e64dbb565b352243/assessments/aipoch-open-science.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2ff0924f0e2ffdc85ce9d0f4e64dbb565b352243\">2ff0924 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-22T04:01:40+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/holt-os/holt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Holt</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-22\">2026-09-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/holt-os/holt/tree/3a88451f980678f5dd62d72d9b159c125c48ae05\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3a88451f980678f5dd62d72d9b159c125c48ae05\">3a88451 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously turn user, Telegram or scheduled objectives into model-selected content/work outcomes with Holt-owned memory and output closure. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous natural-language tasks, recalled workspace context, model-provider responses, recurring scheduled objectives and changing user messages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive answer/task output from the supplied objective, recalled context and skill instructions. - Decision owner: the configured model actor reached through Holt&#39;s first-party direct-API brain path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runApiBrain, runTask, REPL prompt/history construction, memory recall/persistence, routine resolution, OS timers, Telegram polling, file/Telegram output routing and trust/configuration checks. - Closure path: user/schedule/Telegram trigger → Holt prompt + recalled state → direct model decision → returned output → Holt persists task/result memory and/or writes/sends the output → later Holt work can consume the resulting state. - Boundary reachability: direct API brains are a documented standard Holt mode; the built-in REPL uses them when the selected brain has no interactive TUI, and runTask is the shared first-party engine behind holt run, scheduled jobs and Telegram. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model actor owns the material output decision while Holt&#39;s deterministic runtime supplies transport, memory, scheduling and delivery. No Claude Code/Codex/Gemini CLI internals are needed for this positive mapping. - Evidence: src/apibrain.ts; src/commands/chat.ts; src/runner.ts; src/routines.ts; src/scheduler.ts; src/commands/telegram.ts; pinned README direct-API/scheduled-work documentation. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the direct-API path is principally text/model-output agency rather than an unrestricted shell/tool loop; scheduled routines nonetheless close real first-party operational outcomes through persisted output/files/Telegram.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 relation among distinct Holt S1 units is established at the reviewed boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no concrete cross-S1 interference/conflict/oscillation plus attenuation loop was found. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific coordination discretion is supplied. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OS schedules, routine lists, Telegram&#39;s single-busy guard, memory persistence and ordinary task sequencing. - Closure path: no qualifying coordination-result → subsequent sibling-S1 behavior loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: timers, serialization and shared memory order work but are not tied to a demonstrated inter-S1 disturbance that the mechanism exists to damp. - Evidence: src/scheduler.ts; src/commands/schedule.ts; src/commands/telegram.ts; src/runner.ts; routine implementation. - Basis: structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: multiple schedules may coexist, but plurality plus timing is not an S2 witness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: regulate the current Holt installation&#39;s usable operating capacity and automation state across memory, reach, skills, routines/schedules, brain/machine health and related first-party capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current gaps or failures across what Holt knows, what environment it can reach, what repeatable work it can do, what unattended work is configured, and machine/brain/memory conditions that make the installation less capable than intended. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the installation-wide next intervention — for example repair semantic recall, install/remove a hook, add/remove or correct a skill/routine/schedule, change a brain/configuration choice, or stop an unhealthy automation. - Decision owner: the local self-hosted user/operator as legitimate parent actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: /skill audit whole-setup review protocol, holt doctor probes/repair path, memory/voice/hook/skill/routine/schedule/wiki status commands, trust/configuration state and the corresponding mutation/removal commands. - Closure path: current raw installation state → audit report identifies the lowest/broken gap and one corrective action → parent user chooses/runs the first-party control command → Holt changes the affected current capability/configuration/automation → subsequent operation occurs under the returned state. - Boundary reachability: audit ships as a built-in skill and is advertised as the supported way to see what is missing; all cited inspection and corrective commands are ordinary installed Holt CLI surfaces. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit model may recommend a next intervention, but the shipped procedure explicitly leaves the corrective command to the user. The parent decides whether and how current installation capacity changes; deterministic commands merely apply that decision. - Evidence: skills/audit/SKILL.md; src/commands/doctor.ts; src/commands/hook.ts; src/commands/skill.ts; src/commands/routine.ts; src/commands/schedule.ts; README four-rung operating workflow. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: this is an installation-health/current-capability S3 claim, not a claim that every preference or configuration edit is S3. The positive path relies on the shipped whole-setup audit/control ritual rather than isolated settings commands. - Whole-system current view: the built-in audit deliberately spans the four operating rungs — Know, Reach, Do and Run — and requires current evidence from memory/facts, voice, hook, skills, routines, schedules, doctor and wiki state before naming the highest solid rung and lowest gap. - Current-control decision scope: parent intervention over current memory/reach health, available repeatable capabilities, unattended commitments/schedules and the brain/machine conditions that support the installation as a whole.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: challenge ordinary assumptions that the Holt installation is actually healthy and operational by inspecting raw local state through a read-only, cross-surface audit procedure rather than trusting chat or configuration claims. - Disturbance / variety regulated: silent degradation such as keyword-only recall, unembedded memory, hooks that are absent, only built-in rather than work-specific skills, routines that exist but never produced output, broken scheduled work, or automation layered over an empty memory. - Decisive decision or feedback right: assess raw evidence across the four rungs, mark each solid / thin / missing, separate actively broken state from mere absence, and select the single next issue that should enter parent current control. - Decision owner: constructor-level at the reviewed first-party boundary. Holt supplies the audit-specific evidence checklist and judgment protocol, but its own direct-API model mode lacks a first-party tool channel for autonomously executing the required local probes; fully autonomous probe access would require composition or borrowing an external CLI brain, which is outside credited ownership. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only holt memory, memory facts, voice show, hook status, skill list, routine list, schedule list, doctor, wiki status, local logs/status files and the built-in audit prompt. - Closure path: complementary raw state is gathered → the audit protocol forms a strict health finding and one next fix → that finding is returned to the parent S3 path, where the user may apply the corresponding first-party control command. - Boundary reachability: /skill audit is a built-in installed skill in the standard distribution, and every named probe is a first-party Holt command. The constructor classification deliberately avoids treating external Claude/Codex/Gemini shell-tool agency as Holt-owned closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the repository ships a function-specific audit construction rather than generic logs, but autonomous access/judgment closure is not completely first-party in the direct-API mode. This supports C, not A. - Evidence: skills/audit/SKILL.md; src/skills.ts; src/commands/doctor.ts; read-only status/list commands for memory/hooks/skills/routines/schedules/wiki. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a tool-capable external CLI brain may be able to run the probes itself, but its autonomous tool use is excluded by the declared boundary. In direct-API mode the skill explicitly falls back to asking the user to paste raw outputs rather than guessing. - Claim being audited: that the installed Holt setup really has usable Know/Reach/Do/Run capacity and that configured memory/automation is functioning rather than merely existing on disk. - Ordinary reporting path: normal Holt chat/task outputs, existence of configured skills/routines/schedules, and ordinary status/configuration surfaces used during operation. - Complementary access path: the audit procedure reads separate raw local evidence — actual memory facts/mode, hook installation state, skill inventory, routine/schedule inventory, doctor machine/brain/semantic-memory probes, wiki state and, where relevant, produced logs/output. - Independence boundary: the audit is explicitly read-only and checks underlying Holt artifacts/status rather than accepting the ordinary model&#39;s success narrative; however autonomous execution of all probes is not owned by Holt&#39;s direct-API runtime, which is why the state remains constructor-level. - Who acts on findings: the audit returns the lowest gap/breakage and one next command to the local parent operator; that parent owns the S3 corrective intervention.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: adapt future Holt writing behavior from external evidence of how the user actually writes and wants to communicate. - Disturbance / variety regulated: mismatch between generic/model-default prose and the user&#39;s evolving tone, formality, sentence rhythm, formatting, vocabulary, audience and real writing samples. - Decisive decision or feedback right: infer a structured StyleProfile from supplied interview answers and/or real writing samples, including tone/formality/rhythm/formatting/signature moves/banned words/audience and related style distinctions. - Decision owner: the configured model actor reached through Holt&#39;s first-party brain-call path; a direct API brain is sufficient and does not require external CLI-agent tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: holt voice / holt voice add, sample consent/hash/excerpt handling, synthesizeVoice, persistent ~/.holt/voice.json, voicePromptBlock, and holt write generation/self-check path. - Closure path: new user writing/style evidence → model synthesis of a durable structured profile → Holt saves the profile → later holt write loads it into the generation prompt → subsequent operational writing behavior changes under the adaptation. - Boundary reachability: voice interview/add and write are documented standard Holt commands; synthesizeVoice can call a configured direct API brain through first-party runApiBrain, and holt write consumes the saved profile through the same installed runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the user chooses what evidence to share and triggers refresh, but the model actor makes the substantive interpretation from evidence into the structured future-behavior profile and Holt automatically closes that profile into later writing. User input therefore supplies environmental evidence rather than replacing the adaptation judgment. - Evidence: src/voice.ts; src/commands/voice.ts; src/commands/write.ts; src/apibrain.ts; README voice capability. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: fact extraction and wiki synthesis are not separately counted as S4 because they primarily maintain memory. The built-in level-up skill supplies an additional explicitly parent-assisted capability-evolution ritual, but no (P) modifier is claimed here because it is a distinct adaptation path rather than an alternate ownership mode of the voice-synthesis decision used for the autonomous base claim. - External distinction: interview answers and real writing samples expose user-environment distinctions about desired and actual communication style. - Future / prospective distinction: the profile represents how future drafts should change so Holt remains fitted to that style rather than continuing generic model-default writing. - Adaptation option generated: a model-selected structured StyleProfile with concrete prompt-relevant behavior such as tone, formality, sentence length, formatting, signature moves and banned words. - Path back into current capability / S3: the profile is persisted globally and holt write injects it into later generation; the parent S3 operator can inspect, refresh, edit or clear the profile if the adapted behavior is unwanted.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure path is established at the declared Holt-installation recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the review looked for an identity- or ultimate-policy-level issue that can reach legitimate authority and return as a governing decision for subsequent Holt operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no such first-party runtime identity-policy decision path was found. - Decision owner: not established for qualifying S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: static HOLTIDENTITY branding, trust gates, brain/settings selection, onboarding facts/standing rules, voice preferences and user control over skills/routines/schedules. - Closure path: these surfaces configure or constrain ordinary operation but do not form an identity-level issue → authority → authoritative decision → returned organization-wide policy loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: HOLTIDENTITY is developer-authored static prompt/context and therefore cannot establish S5 by itself. User preferences, trust choices and ordinary configuration likewise do not demonstrate ultimate-policy closure. - Evidence: src/commands/launch.ts; skills/onboard/SKILL.md; src/config.ts; trust/settings/voice surfaces inspected alongside standard runtime paths. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Holt strongly brands external interactive brains as Holt and asks onboarding questions about standing rules, but prompt existence and stored preferences are explicitly insufficient for S5 without a runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/223a5595ebd6523b13090510e589aca9f46cabc9/assessments/holt.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 223a5595ebd6523b13090510e589aca9f46cabc9\">223a559 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
